    "https://careers-usu.icims.com/jobs/10817/youth-educator-program-assistant/job": {
      "title": "Youth Educator Program Assistant",
      "description": "Youth Educator Program Assistant City Weber County Posted Date 1 week ago (10/12/2026 6:40 PM) Requisition ID 2026-10817 # of Openings 1 Job Locations US-UT Category (Portal Searching) Extension Position Type Non-benefited (hourly) Part-Time Job Classification Non-Benefited Percent of Time or Hours per Week 19 hrs/week College Extension Department Extension Youth Programs Advertised Salary $19.00/hour Telework Eligible No Overview Utah State University Extension is seeking an organized, enthusiastic, youth-focused individual to serve as a Youth Educator on the Weber County 4-H team. This position will work with the Weber County 4-H team to provide high-quality youth programming throughout the community, with an emphasis on STEM, youth education and hands-on learning opportunities. This position will focus on expanding 4-H outreach while helping youth and families become more engaged in the program. Responsibilities will include organizing and teaching summer Aggie Camps and other educational programs related to STEM that provide engaging, hands-on learning experiences for youth. A key goal will be recruiting and supporting parents and community members as 4-H volunteers, strengthening and growing 4-H clubs, supporting youth and families, coordinating events and activities, and working collaboratively with the Weber County 4-H team to successfully carry out program priorities and expand the reach and impact of 4-H in the community. Position Structure The Youth Educator is one member of the Weber County 4-H team and reports to the Weber County 4-H Program Coordinator. The Program Coordinator provides overall leadership and direction for the Weber County 4-H program, establishes program priorities, coordinates staff responsibilities, and determines how the team's resources can best be used to serve youth and families. The Youth Educator works collaboratively with the Program Coordinator and other 4-H staff to carry out those priorities. Responsibilities may shift throughout the year based on program needs, events, volunteer needs, and the overall team workload. Success in this position is measured not simply by programs personally delivered, but by how effectively the Youth Educator contributes to the success of the entire Weber County 4-H team. This includes coordinating programs and events, supporting youth and families, and helping other team members accomplish shared goals. Responsibilities Weber County 4-H Team Support Work collaboratively as a member of the Weber County 4-H team to accomplish county 4-H goals and priorities Participate in regular team planning and coordination Organize and teach workshops throughout the school year and Aggie Camps throughout the summer months in the community Establish collaborations and form partnerships with other agencies and entities on order to grow the 4-H program in Weber County Help organize and maintain 4-H resources Recruit and assist adult and youth volunteers to teach and build the 4-H program with an emphasis to reach under-served and diverse audiences Coordinate assigned portions of programs and events under the direction of the 4-H Program Coordinator Prepare facilities, supplies, equipment, schedules and other materials Assist with data collection, evaluation and reporting Follow Utah State University, USU Extension, and Utah 4-H policies and procedures, including youth protection, volunteer management, risk management and financial accountability Participate in appropriate USU Extension and Utah 4-H training and professional development Perform other duties as assigned in support of the Weber County 4-H program Qualifications Minimum Qualifications High School graduate At least 6 months experience working with youth, volunteers, education, recreation, community programs, Extension, or related activities Demonstrated ability to work effectively as part of a team Must be available to work in the afternoons during the school year, approximately 1:00 p.m.-8:00 p.m. and weekdays during the summer from 8:00 a.m.- 4:00 p.m. and occasional Saturdays Ability and willingness to travel within Weber County and occasionally to other locations in Utah Must have access to reliable transportation Preferred Qualifications Knowledge of STEM Spanish language proficiency Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Strong interpersonal and communication skills Strong organizational skills and ability to manage multiple activities and deadlines Demonstrate dependability, including arriving on time, meeting deadlines, following through on assignments, and communicating proactively when challenges arise Ability to work independently on assigned responsibilities while maintaining communication and coordination with the larger team. Ability to develop positive working relationships with youth, families, volunteers, and coworkers Basic computer and office technology skills Ability and willingness to learn new programs, processes, and responsibilities A strong commitment to teamwork and collaboration A genuine interest in helping young people learn, grow, and succeed. Effective written and verbal communication Flexibility and willingness to assist in different 4-H program areas based on team needs. Ability to establish positive relationships with youth, parents, volunteers, and coworkers. Ability to organize activities and follow assignments through to completion. Job offer is contingent upon the successful completion of a background check. Required Documents Along with the online application, please attach: Resume to be uploaded at the beginning of your application in the Candidate Profile under “Resume/CV” Cover letter to be typed/pasted at the end of your application **Document size may not exceed 10 MB.** Advertised Salary $19.00/hour ADA Employees work indoors and are protected from weather and/or contaminants, but not, necessarily, occasional temperature changes. This position requires moderate physical activity, including the handling of objects up to 25 pounds and/or standing or walking more than one hour per day. College/Department Highlights About Utah 4-H Utah 4-H is the youth development program of Utah State University Extension. Through clubs, camps, contests, leadership opportunities, and hands-on experiences, 4-H helps young people discover their interests, develop meaningful relationships, build skills, and prepare for work and life. The Weber County 4-H team works together to create meaningful opportunities for young people to explore their interests, develop skills, practice leadership, and gain experiences that prepare them for future success. University Highlights As Utah’s student-centered, land-grant research university, Utah State University expands opportunity and strengthens communities across the state through learning, discovery, extension, and engagement. Founded in 1888, USU now holds two Carnegie distinctions: R1 status for very high research activity and the Carnegie Community Engagement Classification. Its innovative research and scholarship is grounded in reciprocal partnerships with local, regional, and global communities, advancing knowledge and serving the public good across Utah, the nation, and the world. With over 50 world-class research facilities and the nation’s second-oldest undergraduate research program, USU prepares students to become active, civically engaged leaders who can address critical societal challenges. USU enrolls over 29,800 students, both online and in person at locations throughout the state . Utah State’s 30 locations include a main campus in Logan, Utah, residential campuses in Price and Blanding, and six additional statewide campuses, along with education centers serving every county. USU educates students from all 50 states and more than 80 countries. For over 100 years, USU Extension has served and engaged Utahns, serving every county in the state. Competing at the NCAA Division I level in 16 sports, USU is a proud member of the Pac-12 Conference, which it joined July 1, 2026. The Aggies capped a historic final season in the Mountain West by capturing nine combined regular-season and tournament titles in 2025-26, with five teams advancing to postseason play. This tradition of excellence extends to the classroom, where 232 student-athletes earned academic honors, and to the community, where USU ranked second nationally in Division I service hours, with nine teams placing in the top 10 for community service. Utah State is dedicated to fostering a community where all individuals feel respected, valued, and supported and where diversity of thought and culture are cultivated. We seek to recruit, hire, and retain people from all walks of life who will champion excellence in education, research, discovery, outreach, and service. We believe that promoting a strong sense of community and belonging empowers and engages all members of USU to thrive and be successful. Forbes recognized our commitment to employees when it named Utah State the best employer in Utah in 2023. Learn more about USU. The university provides a Dual Career Assistance Program to support careers for partners who are also seeking employment. Additionally, USU is committed to providing access and a reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities. To request a reasonable accommodation for a disability, contact the university’s ADA coordinator in the Office of Human Resources at 435-797-0122 or hr@usu.edu . *updated 10/26 Notice of Non-discrimination In its programs and activities, including in admissions and employment, Utah State University does not discriminate or tolerate discrimination, including harassment, based on race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other status protected by University policy, Title IX, or any other federal, state, or local law. Utah State University is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate or tolerate discrimination including harassment in employment including in hiring, promotion, transfer, or termination based on race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other status protected by University policy or any other federal, state, or local law. Utah State University does not discriminate in its housing offerings and will treat all persons fairly and equally without regard to race, color, religion, sex, familial status, disability, national origin, source of income, sexual orientation, or gender identity. Additionally, the University endeavors to provide reasonable accommodations when necessary and to ensure equal access to qualified persons with disabilities. In all circumstances, Utah State University follows state and federal laws related to sex-segregated spaces. The following office has been designated to handle inquiries regarding the application of Title IX and its implementing regulations and/or USU’s non-discrimination policies: Civil Rights & Title IX Office Old Main Room 405 1475 Old Main Hill, Logan UT 84322 titleix@usu.edu (435) 797-1266 For further information regarding non-discrimination, please visit crtix.usu.edu or contact: U.S. Department of Education Denver Regional Office 303-844-5695| OCR.Denver@ed.gov U.S. Department of Education Office of Assistant Secretary for Civil Rights 800-421-3481 | OCR@ed.gov *updated 08/2026 Options Apply for this job online Apply Share Share this job Refer Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Application FAQs Software Powered by iCIMS"
