    "https://careers-usu.icims.com/jobs/10801/associate-vice-provost-and-director-of-the-center-for-civic-excellence/job": {
      "title": "Associate Vice Provost and Director of the Center for Civic Excellence",
      "description": "Associate Vice Provost and Director of the Center for Civic Excellence City Logan Campus Posted Date 2 weeks ago (10/10/2026 6:44 PM) Requisition ID 2026-10801 # of Openings 1 Job Locations US-UT-Logan Category (Portal Searching) Administrative/Clerical Position Type Benefited Full-Time Job Classification Exempt Percent of Time or Hours per Week 100% College Provost & Executive Vice President Department Center for Civic Excellence Advertised Salary Commensurate with experience plus excellent benefits. Telework Eligible No Overview The Associate Vice Provost & Director of the Center for Civic Excellence provides institutional leadership of the Center for Civic Excellence at Utah State University (USU) which is the administrative unit responsible for overseeing and assessing general education. This role will report to the Vice Provost for Undergraduate Education and serves as the operational lead for the Center for Civic Excellence, chairing the Center for Civic Excellence Curriculum Committee and overseeing the Associate Directors in carrying out the mission of the center and all of its teaching and professional development responsibilities. The Director will lead through shared governance, engaging departments, faculty and Faculty Senate in shaping priorities, assessing program effectiveness, and planning sustainable implementation with colleges and departments. Responsibilities Chair the Center for Civic Excellence Curriculum Committee. Ensure all operations of the Center for Civic Excellence are successfully executed in accordance with the mission, values, and strategic priorities of the Civic Center, including curricular development and governance, assessment, scheduling, concurrent enrollment, professional development, student success initiatives, and website and communications. Work with the Vice Provost for Undergraduate Education and the Division of Student Success to oversee USU’s first-year experience course, USU 1010 – Connections. Hire and supervise Associate Directors to execute the various domains of operational work. Establish a culture of excellence and impact across the Center for Civic Excellence, with students as primary stakeholders. Work with the Vice Provost for Undergraduate Education and Office of Data & Analytics to ensure a data informed approach to instruction and curriculum assessment and review. In support of student and faculty success, interface with the Center for Civic Excellence stakeholders and partners such as, but not limited to, the Office of the Registrar, the Division of Student Success (particularly the Academic Support units), USU Online, Center for Instructional Design and Innovation, etc. Implement the Civic Center’s budget according to set spending priorities. Work with the Vice Provost for Undergraduate Education to ensure coordination with colleges, departments, and other internal and external stakeholders. Contribute to the production of necessary internal and external reporting on the work of the Center for Civic Excellence. Assist the Vice Provost for Undergraduate Education in strategic responsibilities related to the Center for Civic Excellence. Fulfill faculty responsibilities (25% time) consistent with the rank of Associate Professor or full Professor. Other duties as assigned. Qualifications Minimum Qualifications: Terminal degree in a field within the university’s academic mission. 5+ years of experience with teaching and curricular development in general education. Demonstrated impactful leadership in general education, student success, teaching and learning, and academic affairs. Proven ability to collaborate effectively with a broad range of university stakeholders, including academic leadership, faculty, and student support units. Significant experience with general education curriculum, policy, administration, student success, and concurrent enrollment, including demonstrated ability to navigate complex institutional and regulatory requirements. Preferred Qualifications: Strong project management, team building, and communications skills. Commitment to continuous improvement in teaching and student outcomes. Experience with student first year experiences. Required Documents Along with the online application, please attach: Resume to be uploaded at the beginning of your application in the Candidate Profile under “Resume/CV” Cover letter to be typed/pasted at the end of your application **Document size may not exceed 10 MB.** Advertised Salary Commensurate with experience plus excellent benefits. ADA Employees work indoors and are protected from weather and/or contaminants, but not, necessarily, occasional temperature changes. The employee is regularly required to sit and often uses repetitive hand motions. The employee is frequently required to reach with hands and arms and may lift and/or move up to 10 pounds. College/Department Highlights The Center for Civic Excellence is the administrative unit responsible for overseeing and assessing general education at Utah State University. By providing an ambitious foundational education in a program that puts students first, the Civic Center cultivates the intellectual capacities and durable skills that empower students to succeed in college and beyond. Through applied learning and rigorous engagement with big questions, transformative texts, and evidence-based discovery, we prepare thoughtful, capable citizens ready to contribute meaningfully to their communities and the world. Our vision is to deliver the most compelling, intentional, and meaningful common undergraduate experience in the state of Utah and beyond. University Highlights As Utah’s student-centered, land-grant research university, Utah State University expands opportunity and strengthens communities across the state through learning, discovery, extension, and engagement. Founded in 1888, USU now holds two Carnegie distinctions: R1 status for very high research activity and the Carnegie Community Engagement Classification. Its innovative research and scholarship is grounded in reciprocal partnerships with local, regional, and global communities, advancing knowledge and serving the public good across Utah, the nation, and the world. With over 50 world-class research facilities and the nation’s second-oldest undergraduate research program, USU prepares students to become active, civically engaged leaders who can address critical societal challenges. USU enrolls over 29,800 students, both online and in person at locations throughout the state . Utah State’s 30 locations include a main campus in Logan, Utah, residential campuses in Price and Blanding, and six additional statewide campuses, along with education centers serving every county. USU educates students from all 50 states and more than 80 countries. For over 100 years, USU Extension has served and engaged Utahns, serving every county in the state. Competing at the NCAA Division I level in 16 sports, USU is a proud member of the Pac-12 Conference, which it joined July 1, 2026. The Aggies capped a historic final season in the Mountain West by capturing nine combined regular-season and tournament titles in 2025-26, with five teams advancing to postseason play. This tradition of excellence extends to the classroom, where 232 student-athletes earned academic honors, and to the community, where USU ranked second nationally in Division I service hours, with nine teams placing in the top 10 for community service. Utah State is dedicated to fostering a community where all individuals feel respected, valued, and supported and where diversity of thought and culture are cultivated. We seek to recruit, hire, and retain people from all walks of life who will champion excellence in education, research, discovery, outreach, and service. We believe that promoting a strong sense of community and belonging empowers and engages all members of USU to thrive and be successful. Forbes recognized our commitment to employees when it named Utah State the best employer in Utah in 2023. Learn more about USU. The university provides a Dual Career Assistance Program to support careers for partners who are also seeking employment. Additionally, USU is committed to providing access and a reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities. To request a reasonable accommodation for a disability, contact the university’s ADA coordinator in the Office of Human Resources at 435-797-0122 or hr@usu.edu . *updated 10/26 Notice of Non-discrimination In its programs and activities, including in admissions and employment, Utah State University does not discriminate or tolerate discrimination, including harassment, based on race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other status protected by University policy, Title IX, or any other federal, state, or local law. Utah State University is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate or tolerate discrimination including harassment in employment including in hiring, promotion, transfer, or termination based on race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other status protected by University policy or any other federal, state, or local law. Utah State University does not discriminate in its housing offerings and will treat all persons fairly and equally without regard to race, color, religion, sex, familial status, disability, national origin, source of income, sexual orientation, or gender identity. Additionally, the University endeavors to provide reasonable accommodations when necessary and to ensure equal access to qualified persons with disabilities. In all circumstances, Utah State University follows state and federal laws related to sex-segregated spaces. The following office has been designated to handle inquiries regarding the application of Title IX and its implementing regulations and/or USU’s non-discrimination policies: Civil Rights & Title IX Office Old Main Room 405 1475 Old Main Hill, Logan UT 84322 titleix@usu.edu (435) 797-1266 For further information regarding non-discrimination, please visit crtix.usu.edu or contact: U.S. Department of Education Denver Regional Office 303-844-5695| OCR.Denver@ed.gov U.S. Department of Education Office of Assistant Secretary for Civil Rights 800-421-3481 | OCR@ed.gov *updated 08/2026 Options Apply for this job online Apply Share Share this job Refer Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Application FAQs Software Powered by iCIMS"
    },
    "https://careers-usu.icims.com/jobs/10809/program-coordinator-ii/job": {
      "title": "Program Coordinator II",
      "description": "Program Coordinator II City Tooele Campus Posted Date 2 weeks ago (10/10/2026 5:17 PM) Requisition ID 2026-10809 # of Openings 1 Job Locations US-UT-Tooele Category (Portal Searching) Extension Position Type Benefited Full-Time Job Classification Exempt Percent of Time or Hours per Week 100% College Extension Department Extension Home & Community Advertised Salary $53,000 - $60,000 Telework Eligible No Overview The Peer Support Outreach Coordinator is the primary field-facing position for USU Extension's Rural Peer Support Internship Program, funded by the Utah Department of Health and Human Services (DHHS) through the Utah Rural Health Transformation Program (RHTP). The program helps rural healthcare and community agencies across seven Utah health districts understand, prepare for, and integrate peer support services, and places funded peer support interns with competitively selected host agencies. This position has two primary functions: (1) conducting outreach and technical assistance to agencies across rural Utah and building the relationships that bring them into the program and keep them engaged through each phase; and (2) developing educational materials that teach agencies what peer support is, what Certified Peer Support Specialists (CPSS) and Family Peer Support Specialists (FPSS) do, and how peer services can be integrated into their operations. The coordinator also schedules and conducts site visits, coordinates the delivery of coaching and education, supports agencies through the internship application process, enrolls peer interns once host agencies are selected, and collects program data. Job offer is contingent upon the successful completion of a background check Responsibilities Agency outreach and engagement (primary function). Identifies and proactively reaches out to rural healthcare and community agencies, such as hospitals, primary care clinics, federally qualified health centers, local mental health and substance use authorities, treatment providers, and community-based organizations, across the seven participating health districts. Introduces the peer support internship program, engages agency leadership, and builds and maintains the trusted working relationships needed to bring agencies into the program and keep them engaged through each phase. Educational materials development (primary function). Designs, writes, and produces educational materials that teach agencies what peer support is and how to integrate it, including presentations, fact sheets, one-pagers, printed educational packets, guides, and digital content. Topics include the roles of CPSS and FPSS, the evidence base for peer support, supervision and workforce considerations, and pathways for sustaining peer positions. Works with the Peer Support Technical Expert & Coach to translate the program's implementation toolkit into clear, accessible materials tailored to rural agency administrators, clinicians, and staff. Education and presentations. Presents to agency leadership, staff, boards, and community coalitions on peer support and the internship program, both in person and virtually. Represents USU Extension at community meetings, coalitions, and professional events. Site visits. Schedules, plans, and conducts site visits with candidate and participating agencies throughout Utah. Assesses agency interest and readiness to host peer support workers, and documents visits and follow-up actions. Coaching and education coordination. Coordinates the delivery of coaching, education, and technical assistance to agencies, including scheduling, logistics, materials, and follow-up. Tracks each agency's progress in adopting the program's implementation toolkit and plan. Internship application support. Guides candidate agencies through the competitive internship application process. Explains eligibility, requirements, and timelines; answers questions; helps agencies prepare complete and competitive applications; and supports the program's selection process. Peer intern recruitment and enrollment. Promotes the internship to prospective peer support interns. Once host agencies are selected, coordinates intern enrollment, onboarding, and placement, and serves as a liaison among interns, host agencies, and USU Extension. Sustainability guidance. Helps agencies understand options for sustaining peer support positions beyond the internship, including Medicaid reimbursement for peer support services, and connects them to appropriate resources. Data collection and records. Collects program data in the field (e.g., agency engagement, site visits, toolkit adoption, intern enrollment, and peer service utilization) using established instruments. Maintains accurate contact, activity, and program records, and works with the Data Manager & Evaluator to meet DHHS reporting requirements and deadlines. Program promotion. Develops content for social media, websites, and other outreach channels to promote the program and recruit host agencies and peer interns. Program operations. Assists with developing partner agreements (e.g., MOUs), standard operating procedures, and operational plans. Contributes to program planning and operational decisions, and participates in coordination meetings with DHHS as requested. Administrative support. Manages program correspondence, scheduling, travel arrangements, and purchasing for outreach and educational materials. Monitors outreach-related expenses such as travel and printing. Professional development. Attends relevant trainings and conferences (e.g., the Utah Fall Conference on Substance Use) and may present program lessons learned. May train and direct student employees, interns, and volunteers. Performs miscellaneous job-related duties as assigned. Qualifications Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in a related field plus two years of relevant work experience; or a master’s degree in a related field; or an equivalent combination of education plus experience is required. Related fields include social work, public health, psychology, human services, health education, communication, or a closely related field. Access to reliable transportation and the ability to travel regularly throughout Utah, including overnight stays Preferred Qualifications: Current or previous certification as a Certified Peer Support Specialist (CPSS) or Family Peer Support Specialist (FPSS) in Utah. Lived experience of recovery from mental health and/or substance use challenges, personally or as a family member, and comfort drawing on that experience professionally. Experience in outreach, community engagement, provider relations, or partnership development with healthcare, behavioral health, or community organizations. Experience developing educational, training, or promotional materials. Experience living or working in rural or frontier Utah communities. Working knowledge of Medicaid reimbursement for peer support services. Experience coordinating or collecting data for grant-funded projects. Required Documents Along with the online application, please attach: Resume to be uploaded at the beginning of your application in the Candidate Profile under “Resume/CV” Contact Informaiton for 3 Professional References to be typed at the beginning of the application. Answer the following question within the application This position requires regular travel to fulfill job responsibilities. Do you have access to reliable transportation to meet this requirement? Travel expenses are reimbursed. **Document size may not exceed 10 MB.** Advertised Salary $53,000 - $60,000 ADA Work Environment and Physical Requirements This position is based at the USU Extension office in Tooele County (Tooele, Utah). Regular in-state travel is required, including day trips and overnight stays, to conduct outreach and site visits in all seven participating health districts (Bear River, Central Utah, San Juan, Southeast Utah, Southwest Utah, TriCounty, and Tooele County) and to attend conferences and meetings. The incumbent must be able to drive long distances, including on rural roads in variable weather conditions. Occasional evening or weekend work may be required to accommodate community meetings and events. Employees typically work in an office environment and are protected from weather conditions or contaminants, but not necessarily occasional temperature changes. However, in certain instances employees may work outdoors and may not be protected from weather conditions. Employees could be exposed to hazardous chemicals. Employees nearly continuously sit and often use repetitive hand motion (such as typing). Bending and stooping is occasionally required. Employees nearly continuously listen, hear, and talk. University Highlights As Utah’s student-centered, land-grant research university, Utah State University expands opportunity and strengthens communities across the state through learning, discovery, extension, and engagement. Founded in 1888, USU now holds two Carnegie distinctions: R1 status for very high research activity and the Carnegie Community Engagement Classification. Its innovative research and scholarship is grounded in reciprocal partnerships with local, regional, and global communities, advancing knowledge and serving the public good across Utah, the nation, and the world. With over 50 world-class research facilities and the nation’s second-oldest undergraduate research program, USU prepares students to become active, civically engaged leaders who can address critical societal challenges. USU enrolls over 29,800 students, both online and in person at locations throughout the state . Utah State’s 30 locations include a main campus in Logan, Utah, residential campuses in Price and Blanding, and six additional statewide campuses, along with education centers serving every county. USU educates students from all 50 states and more than 80 countries. For over 100 years, USU Extension has served and engaged Utahns, serving every county in the state. Competing at the NCAA Division I level in 16 sports, USU is a proud member of the Pac-12 Conference, which it joined July 1, 2026. The Aggies capped a historic final season in the Mountain West by capturing nine combined regular-season and tournament titles in 2025-26, with five teams advancing to postseason play. This tradition of excellence extends to the classroom, where 232 student-athletes earned academic honors, and to the community, where USU ranked second nationally in Division I service hours, with nine teams placing in the top 10 for community service. Utah State is dedicated to fostering a community where all individuals feel respected, valued, and supported and where diversity of thought and culture are cultivated. We seek to recruit, hire, and retain people from all walks of life who will champion excellence in education, research, discovery, outreach, and service. We believe that promoting a strong sense of community and belonging empowers and engages all members of USU to thrive and be successful. Forbes recognized our commitment to employees when it named Utah State the best employer in Utah in 2023. Learn more about USU. The university provides a Dual Career Assistance Program to support careers for partners who are also seeking employment. Additionally, USU is committed to providing access and a reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities. To request a reasonable accommodation for a disability, contact the university’s ADA coordinator in the Office of Human Resources at 435-797-0122 or hr@usu.edu . *updated 10/26 Notice of Non-discrimination In its programs and activities, including in admissions and employment, Utah State University does not discriminate or tolerate discrimination, including harassment, based on race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other status protected by University policy, Title IX, or any other federal, state, or local law. Utah State University is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate or tolerate discrimination including harassment in employment including in hiring, promotion, transfer, or termination based on race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other status protected by University policy or any other federal, state, or local law. Utah State University does not discriminate in its housing offerings and will treat all persons fairly and equally without regard to race, color, religion, sex, familial status, disability, national origin, source of income, sexual orientation, or gender identity. Additionally, the University endeavors to provide reasonable accommodations when necessary and to ensure equal access to qualified persons with disabilities. In all circumstances, Utah State University follows state and federal laws related to sex-segregated spaces. The following office has been designated to handle inquiries regarding the application of Title IX and its implementing regulations and/or USU’s non-discrimination policies: Civil Rights & Title IX Office Old Main Room 405 1475 Old Main Hill, Logan UT 84322 titleix@usu.edu (435) 797-1266 For further information regarding non-discrimination, please visit crtix.usu.edu or contact: U.S. Department of Education Denver Regional Office 303-844-5695| OCR.Denver@ed.gov U.S. Department of Education Office of Assistant Secretary for Civil Rights 800-421-3481 | OCR@ed.gov *updated 08/2026 Options Apply for this job online Apply Share Share this job Refer Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Application FAQs Software Powered by iCIMS"
