    "https://careers-usu.icims.com/jobs/10787/assistant-professor---behavioral-neuroscience/job": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Behavioral Neuroscience",
      "description": "Assistant Professor - Behavioral Neuroscience City Logan Campus Posted Date 2 weeks ago (10/9/2026 12:59 PM) Requisition ID 2026-10787 # of Openings 1 Job Locations US-UT-Logan Category (Portal Searching) Faculty Position Type Benefited Full-Time Job Classification Faculty Percent of Time or Hours per Week 100% College EEJ Coll of Educ Hlth & Human Sci Department Psychology Advertised Salary Commensurate with education and experience, plus excellent benefits. Telework Eligible No Overview The Psychology Department at Utah State University invites applications for two tenure-track Assistant Professor positions in Behavioral Neuroscience. We are interested in candidates whose research programs advance understanding of brain–behavior relationships using behavioral, cognitive, developmental, affective, computational, or translational neuroscience approaches. We encourage applications from all qualified candidates whose work aligns with behavioral neuroscience broadly construed. The department newly offers a B.S. in Behavioral Neuroscience and is actively expanding the program’s curriculum and research activities. The successful candidate will teach undergraduate courses within this program, as well as advanced graduate seminars. We are particularly interested in candidates whose research program provides meaningful opportunities for undergraduate participation. We seek new colleagues who are committed to involving students in research through laboratory experiences, thesis supervision, conference presentations, and other high-impact learning opportunities. To support these endeavors, the Department offers a supportive research environment, including dedicated laboratory space, specialized equipment, and facilities that support both human and non-human animal research. Located on the main campus in Logan, Utah, the positions are tenure-track, full-time, nine-month academic year appointments and are not eligible for remote work. Start date for the positions will be August 1, 2027. The positions are contingent on a successful background check. The Department of Psychology will consider visa sponsorship. Candidates must have valid U.S. work authorization (e.g., F-1 OPT/STEM OPT, J-1, current H-1B), be eligible for Change of Status (e.g. F-1, J-1), or be eligible for an H-1B transfer. We do not provide sponsorship for initial overseas H-1B filings/fees. Review of applications will begin November 10, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Inquiries may be directed to the search committee chair, Dr. Christopher Warren ( chris.warren@usu.edu ). Responsibilities Conduct and disseminate research in behavioral neuroscience (e.g., develop an independent research program, publish in peer-reviewed journals) Establish an active research program with the potential to attract external funding and that actively involves undergraduate and graduate students. Contribute to undergraduate and graduate instruction, including the continued development of the B.S. in Behavioral Neuroscience, through courses aligned with the candidate’s expertise and departmental needs. Mentor graduate students in psychology and/or the interdisciplinary Neuroscience PhD program Participate in departmental governance, including committee work and program development Contribute service to the college, university, and profession Qualifications Minimum Qualifications: Completed PhD in Psychology, Neuroscience, or a closely related field before appointment (ABD considered with a timely completion plan) Active or developing research program in behavioral neuroscience Evidence of research productivity and potential to secure external research funding Effective teaching and mentoring ability Strong oral, written, and interpersonal communication skills Preferred Qualifications: Demonstrated potential to engage undergraduate and graduate students in research, professional, clinical, community-engaged, or other experiential opportunities that support their academic and career development Evidence of grant involvement or success Research program complementing or extending departmental and university neuroscience strengths Potential to contribute to the growth of the undergraduate Behavioral Neuroscience program, including development of training that prepares students for post-baccalaureate education and training, and/or careers in neuroscience, health, human-technology interaction, or related fields Ability to teach graduate-level methods or quantitative coursework Evidence of effective supervision or mentorship Experience working collaboratively across disciplinary contexts Required Documents Along with the online application, please attach: A curriculum vitae to be uploaded at the beginning of your application in the Candidate Profile under “Resume/CV” A cover letter highlighting fit with minimum and preferred qualifications to be uploaded at the beginning of your application in the Candidate Profile under “Documents 1-10” Three representative publications to be uploaded at the beginning of your application in the Candidate Profile under “Documents 1-10” Names and contact information for three (3) professional references to be entered in the Candidate Profile **Document size may not exceed 10 MB.* Advertised Salary Commensurate with education and experience, plus excellent benefits. ADA Employees typically work indoors and are protected from weather and/or contaminants, but not, necessarily, occasional temperature changes. College/Department Highlights The Department of Psychology at Utah State University is a research-intensive department within the Emma Eccles Jones College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences, with strengths spanning basic, translational, and applied psychological science. The department is entering a period of significant faculty growth, with five tenure-track faculty hires planned across four searches in 2026–27. The department includes more than 30 faculty and offers doctoral training through four Ph.D. specializations: Behavior Analysis, Brain and Cognition, Combined Clinical/Counseling Psychology, and School Psychology. Department faculty also contribute to USU’s interdisciplinary Ph.D. program in Neuroscience. At the undergraduate level, the department offers B.S. degrees in Psychology and Behavioral Neuroscience, as well as an award-winning online Psychology program. The department also offers a distance-learning M.Ed. in Professional School Counseling. Faculty maintain active programs of research supported by federal and other external funding and collaborate extensively within the department, across the college, and throughout the university. Existing collaborations connect Psychology faculty with colleagues in areas including biology, human development and family studies, special education and rehabilitation counseling, nursing, speech and hearing sciences, kinesiology, economics, mathematics and statistics, sociology, and natural resources. The department values research that crosses traditional disciplinary boundaries and creates opportunities for collaboration in areas such as behavioral and mental health, neuroscience, health and aging, intervention science, and emerging health technologies. The department is committed to excellence in undergraduate and graduate education, research and scholarship, and service to communities in Utah and beyond. Faculty joining the department during this period of growth will have opportunities to help shape new research collaborations, academic programs, and training initiatives while contributing to established programs with strong records of student mentorship, research productivity, and professional impact. The Emma Eccles Jones College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences is dedicated to excellence in research, teaching, and service activities that advance education, improve health and well-being, and elevate human potential across the lifespan. The college is home to eight academic departments with approximately 200 faculty who are engaged in innovative research and impactful, student-focused graduate and undergraduate education. The college is consistently ranked by US News and World Report as one of the top Graduate Colleges of Education in the country, ranking among the top 30 public institutions of higher education in 2026 and 1st in the state of Utah since 2000. The college’s programs and outreach missions are strengthened by an on-campus elementary laboratory school and five stand-alone centers, including the Sorenson Legacy Foundation Center for Clinical Excellence , Alzheimer’s Disease and Dementia Research Center , Center for the School of the Future , Institute for Disability Research, Policy, & Practice , and National Center for Hearing Assessment & Management . Faculty, students, and staff within the college collaborate with a broad range of community partners to facilitate community-engaged research and provide transformative experiential learning opportunities. Through dedicated teaching, innovative research, and meaningful engagement the Emma Eccles Jones College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences advances education, health, and well-being, enriching the lives of individuals, families, and communities throughout Utah. University Highlights As Utah’s student-centered, land-grant research university, Utah State University expands opportunity and strengthens communities across the state through learning, discovery, extension, and engagement. Founded in 1888, USU now holds two Carnegie distinctions: R1 status for very high research activity and the Carnegie Community Engagement Classification. Its innovative research and scholarship is grounded in reciprocal partnerships with local, regional, and global communities, advancing knowledge and serving the public good across Utah, the nation, and the world. With over 50 world-class research facilities and the nation’s second-oldest undergraduate research program, USU prepares students to become active, civically engaged leaders who can address critical societal challenges. USU enrolls over 29,800 students, both online and in person at locations throughout the state . Utah State’s 30 locations include a main campus in Logan, Utah, residential campuses in Price and Blanding, and six additional statewide campuses, along with education centers serving every county. USU educates students from all 50 states and more than 80 countries. For over 100 years, USU Extension has served and engaged Utahns, serving every county in the state. Competing at the NCAA Division I level in 16 sports, USU is a proud member of the Pac-12 Conference, which it joined July 1, 2026. The Aggies capped a historic final season in the Mountain West by capturing nine combined regular-season and tournament titles in 2025-26, with five teams advancing to postseason play. This tradition of excellence extends to the classroom, where 232 student-athletes earned academic honors, and to the community, where USU ranked second nationally in Division I service hours, with nine teams placing in the top 10 for community service. Utah State is dedicated to fostering a community where all individuals feel respected, valued, and supported and where diversity of thought and culture are cultivated. We seek to recruit, hire, and retain people from all walks of life who will champion excellence in education, research, discovery, outreach, and service. We believe that promoting a strong sense of community and belonging empowers and engages all members of USU to thrive and be successful. Forbes recognized our commitment to employees when it named Utah State the best employer in Utah in 2023. Learn more about USU. The university provides a Dual Career Assistance Program to support careers for partners who are also seeking employment. Additionally, USU is committed to providing access and a reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities. To request a reasonable accommodation for a disability, contact the university’s ADA coordinator in the Office of Human Resources at 435-797-0122 or hr@usu.edu . *updated 10/26 Notice of Non-discrimination In its programs and activities, including in admissions and employment, Utah State University does not discriminate or tolerate discrimination, including harassment, based on race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other status protected by University policy, Title IX, or any other federal, state, or local law. Utah State University is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate or tolerate discrimination including harassment in employment including in hiring, promotion, transfer, or termination based on race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other status protected by University policy or any other federal, state, or local law. Utah State University does not discriminate in its housing offerings and will treat all persons fairly and equally without regard to race, color, religion, sex, familial status, disability, national origin, source of income, sexual orientation, or gender identity. Additionally, the University endeavors to provide reasonable accommodations when necessary and to ensure equal access to qualified persons with disabilities. In all circumstances, Utah State University follows state and federal laws related to sex-segregated spaces. The following office has been designated to handle inquiries regarding the application of Title IX and its implementing regulations and/or USU’s non-discrimination policies: Civil Rights & Title IX Office Old Main Room 405 1475 Old Main Hill, Logan UT 84322 titleix@usu.edu (435) 797-1266 For further information regarding non-discrimination, please visit crtix.usu.edu or contact: U.S. Department of Education Denver Regional Office 303-844-5695| OCR.Denver@ed.gov U.S. Department of Education Office of Assistant Secretary for Civil Rights 800-421-3481 | OCR@ed.gov *updated 08/2026 Options Apply for this job online Apply Share Share this job Refer Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Application FAQs Software Powered by iCIMS"
    },
    "https://careers-usu.icims.com/jobs/10761/training-dev-specialist-ii/job": {
      "title": "Training/Dev Specialist II",
      "description": "Training/Dev Specialist II City Salt Lake City Campus Posted Date 2 weeks ago (10/7/2026 6:34 PM) Requisition ID 2026-10761 # of Openings 1 Job Locations US-UT-Taylorsville Category (Portal Searching) Other Position Type Benefited Full-Time Job Classification Exempt Percent of Time or Hours per Week 100% Time College EEJ Coll of Educ Hlth & Human Sci Department Inst for Disabil Res Policy & Pract Advertised Salary Minimum $60,000; Commensurate with experience plus excellent benefits. Telework Eligible Hybrid Overview The Training/Dev Specialist II serves as a subject matter expert and uses technical knowledge to develop curriculum and to design and deliver training for disability support professionals, families, and other stakeholders. The Training/Development Specialist II must have the knowledge and skills to develop synchronous and asynchronous formats. This position also produces and edits instructional videos and implements project policies and guidelines. This position reports to the IDRPP Research and Training Division Director and has no supervisory responsibilities. This position is eligible for hybrid telework in the state of Utah. Exempt positions are eligible for excellent benefits including: 13 paid holidays, earn 22 days of paid vacation leave and 12 days of sick leave per year, 50% tuition reduction for employees and dependents, competitive health benefits packages and a generous contribution into your retirement. View more information here: Summary of Benefits 2025-2026 . Responsibilities Consults as a subject matter expert in the assigned area. Designs, develops, and evaluates curriculum and instructional materials for adult learners, including disability support professionals, families, and other stakeholders, applying adult learning principles in both synchronous (live, instructor-led) and asynchronous (self-paced, online) formats. Plans, writes, and produces training videos, including scripting, storyboarding, recording, and screen capture. Edits videos and related multimedia, including adding captions, graphics, and narration, and ensures all media meet accessibility standards. Delivers live training to disability support professionals, families, and other audiences in one-on-one, on-site, virtual, and group formats, including workshops, webinars, and seminars. Develops and evaluates products for disability professionals and families Evaluates training effectiveness through learner feedback, assessments, and outcome data, and uses the results to revise content and delivery. Ensures that training and consulting processes are adequately documented. Assists in developing reports and proposals. Collaborates on presentations, publications, and proposals. Writes articles, blogs, tip sheets, and other media as requested. Implements project policies and guidelines. Designs, implements, executes, and completes multiple local, state, and federal projects. Generates new and innovative project ideas. May write and submit grant proposals. Disseminates project outcomes and results through statewide and national presentations and related publications. Serves on advisory boards and councils as requested. Attends meetings, trainings, and conferences as requested. Performs miscellaneous job-related duties as assigned. Qualifications Minimum Qualifications A bachelor's degree in a relevant field plus two years of relevant experience. Preferred Qualifications A master's degree in a relevant field (such as education, rehabilitation counseling, special education, instructional design, or a related discipline), plus experience designing adult training, producing and editing instructional video, and working in special education, disability services, or education-related programs. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Knowledge of disability support systems. Knowledge of instructional design models and adult learning theory, and skill in applying them to build effective training. Skill in developing online and blended learning content, including using a learning management system (LMS) and authoring tools. Skill in recording, editing, and producing training videos using video editing and screen-capture software. Knowledge of accessibility standards (such as Section 508 and WCAG) for digital content, video, and captioning. Skill in facilitating engaging live training, both in person and virtual. Skill in adapting training content and communication for audiences with varied backgrounds, roles, and levels of experience, including families and non-professional learners. Skill with computers, related software, and other technologies. Ability to communicate across formats, including phone, email, and professional presentation. Ability to deliver presentations to diverse audiences. Skill with databases and data analysis, including using data to evaluate training outcomes. Required Documents Along with the online application, please attach: Resume/CV to be uploaded at the beginning of your application in the Candidate Profile under “Resume/CV\". Cover Letter to be uploaded at the beginning of your application in the Candidate Profile under “Documents 1-10”. This letter should outline how you meet the minimum qualifications, with specific examples from your education and/or work experience. Applications submitted without a cover letter that addresses the minimum qualifications will not be considered. **Document size may not exceed 10 MB.** Advertised Salary Minimum $60,000; Commensurate with experience plus excellent benefits. ADA Frequent statewide travel, including travel to urban, rural, and frontier communities, is required; occasional overnight travel is expected. A valid driver’s license and the ability to travel independently throughout the state are required. College/Department Highlights The Institute for Disability Research, Policy & Practice (IDRPP) is Utah’s federally designated University Center for Excellence in Developmental Disabilities (UCEDD). The IDRPP opened its doors in 1972 and was the first UCEDD program in the U.S. to focus on the educational and social needs of children and adults with disabilities. Over the past 50 years the IDRPP has grown into one of the largest UCEDD programs in the nation and is recognized as a pioneer in research, training, technical assistance, and services for children and adults with disabilities, their families, and the professionals who serve them. The IDRPP is committed to hiring people with disabilities, veterans, and diverse individuals from all communities and backgrounds. We value visible and invisible differences, and we recognize that diversity makes us stronger. We welcome applicants who bring something different and unique to our organization. We value creativity, loyalty, and hard work. We believe that we can change the world and make it a better, more inclusive place for everyone, and we want to hire people who also believe that they can make a difference. Mission: We are working together to create inclusive communities and improve the lives of children and adults with disabilities and their families through sustainable innovation, collaborative research, responsive service, and interdisciplinary training and education. Values: We Innovate: We lead by developing dynamic, sustainable, forward-thinking programs and services. We Include: We demonstrate how to effectively work toward inclusive communities in our planning, programs, and services. We celebrate diversity in ability, race, ethnicity, culture, language, gender, sexual orientation, politics, and religion. We Collaborate: We forge partnerships. Working together makes us more effective and responsive to the current needs of our clients and communities. We Care: We do what we do because we care. We are making the world a better place for people with disabilities and their families. Funding Statement The Institute for Disability Research, Policy, & Practice is one of the largest research and outreach centers at USU, and we are largely funded through grants, contracts, and fee-for-service programs. All staff positions at the IDRPP are contingent upon funding, and all senior staff at the IDRPP with graduate degrees are responsible for helping to generate funding to support our operations and growth. College Highlights The Emma Eccles Jones College of Education and Human Services is home to eight departments and is committed to providing the best learning opportunities and educational research in the state of Utah. Our programs are strengthened by an on-campus elementary laboratory school and five stand-alone centers, including the Sorenson Legacy Foundation Center for Clinical Excellence. The college offers clinical services to the community across the human lifespan and provides students with real-world service and research opportunities. University Highlights As Utah’s student-centered, land-grant research university, Utah State University expands opportunity and strengthens communities across the state through learning, discovery, extension, and engagement. Founded in 1888, USU now holds two Carnegie distinctions: R1 status for very high research activity and the Carnegie Community Engagement Classification. Its innovative research and scholarship is grounded in reciprocal partnerships with local, regional, and global communities, advancing knowledge and serving the public good across Utah, the nation, and the world. With over 50 world-class research facilities and the nation’s second-oldest undergraduate research program, USU prepares students to become active, civically engaged leaders who can address critical societal challenges. USU enrolls over 29,800 students, both online and in person at locations throughout the state . Utah State’s 30 locations include a main campus in Logan, Utah, residential campuses in Price and Blanding, and six additional statewide campuses, along with education centers serving every county. USU educates students from all 50 states and more than 80 countries. For over 100 years, USU Extension has served and engaged Utahns, serving every county in the state. Competing at the NCAA Division I level in 16 sports, USU is a proud member of the Pac-12 Conference, which it joined July 1, 2026. The Aggies capped a historic final season in the Mountain West by capturing nine combined regular-season and tournament titles in 2025-26, with five teams advancing to postseason play. This tradition of excellence extends to the classroom, where 232 student-athletes earned academic honors, and to the community, where USU ranked second nationally in Division I service hours, with nine teams placing in the top 10 for community service. Utah State is dedicated to fostering a community where all individuals feel respected, valued, and supported and where diversity of thought and culture are cultivated. We seek to recruit, hire, and retain people from all walks of life who will champion excellence in education, research, discovery, outreach, and service. We believe that promoting a strong sense of community and belonging empowers and engages all members of USU to thrive and be successful. Forbes recognized our commitment to employees when it named Utah State the best employer in Utah in 2023. Learn more about USU. The university provides a Dual Career Assistance Program to support careers for partners who are also seeking employment. Additionally, USU is committed to providing access and a reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities. To request a reasonable accommodation for a disability, contact the university’s ADA coordinator in the Office of Human Resources at 435-797-0122 or hr@usu.edu . *updated 10/26 Notice of Non-discrimination In its programs and activities, including in admissions and employment, Utah State University does not discriminate or tolerate discrimination, including harassment, based on race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other status protected by University policy, Title IX, or any other federal, state, or local law. Utah State University is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate or tolerate discrimination including harassment in employment including in hiring, promotion, transfer, or termination based on race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other status protected by University policy or any other federal, state, or local law. Utah State University does not discriminate in its housing offerings and will treat all persons fairly and equally without regard to race, color, religion, sex, familial status, disability, national origin, source of income, sexual orientation, or gender identity. Additionally, the University endeavors to provide reasonable accommodations when necessary and to ensure equal access to qualified persons with disabilities. In all circumstances, Utah State University follows state and federal laws related to sex-segregated spaces. The following office has been designated to handle inquiries regarding the application of Title IX and its implementing regulations and/or USU’s non-discrimination policies: Civil Rights & Title IX Office Old Main Room 405 1475 Old Main Hill, Logan UT 84322 titleix@usu.edu (435) 797-1266 For further information regarding non-discrimination, please visit crtix.usu.edu or contact: U.S. Department of Education Denver Regional Office 303-844-5695| OCR.Denver@ed.gov U.S. Department of Education Office of Assistant Secretary for Civil Rights 800-421-3481 | OCR@ed.gov *updated 08/2026 Options Apply for this job online Apply Share Share this job Refer Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Application FAQs Software Powered by iCIMS"
