    "https://careers-usu.icims.com/jobs/10790/part-time-student-success-mentor/job": {
      "title": "Part-Time Student Success Mentor",
      "description": "Part-Time Student Success Mentor City Blanding Campus Posted Date 2 weeks ago (10/5/2026 4:26 PM) Requisition ID 2026-10790 # of Openings 3 Job Locations US-UT-Blanding Category (Portal Searching) Student Programs & Services Position Type Non-benefited (hourly) Part-Time Job Classification Non-Benefited Percent of Time or Hours per Week Up to 25 hours a week College Statewide & Online Education Department SW Campuses RES-Blanding Advertised Salary $18.00 to $20.00 an hour. Commensurate on experience and education. Telework Eligible No Overview Utah State University (USU) is seeking a dedicated and relationship-focused Mentor to support students at the USU Blanding campus and extend mentoring and student-support services into the surrounding communities. The Mentor will build meaningful relationships with students, help them navigate college life and academic expectations, and connect them with campus and community resources. A key component of this position is outreach to prospective and current students in rural and surrounding communities, helping strengthen pathways to higher education and increasing awareness of the educational opportunities available through USU. This position requires someone who is comfortable working both on campus and in community settings, building trust with students, families, schools, tribal communities, and community partners. Regular travel to outreach locations will be expected. This is a 9-month position, based on refunding opportunities. Responsibilities Provide consistent, supportive, and culturally responsive mentoring to USU Blanding students. Become a source of encouragement and support to students at risk of academic failure as identified in the Early Alert System. Develop trusting relationships with students and serve as a reliable source of encouragement, guidance, and support. Help students identify academic, personal, and career goals and develop strategies to achieve them. Assist students in navigating university resources, services, policies, and processes. Connect students with appropriate academic advising, tutoring, financial aid, mental health, career, and other support services. Monitor student engagement and progress and proactively reach out to students who may need additional support. Conduct outreach activities in Monticello, Montezuma Creek, Monument Valley, and surrounding communities. Build and maintain positive relationships with local schools, community organizations, tribal/community partners, families, and other stakeholders. Participate in college-access events, workshops, presentations, recruitment activities, and community events. Help prospective students understand USU programs, admissions processes, financial aid, and available student-support resources. Support transition and retention efforts for students entering USU from local communities. Coordinate with USU Blanding faculty, staff, advisors, recruiters, and other student-support personnel. Maintain accurate records of mentoring and outreach activities while protecting student confidentiality. Travel regularly between USU Blanding and designated outreach communities. Attend staff meetings, professional-development activities, and other university events as required. Perform other duties related to student mentoring, outreach, and college-access initiatives as assigned. Qualifications Minimum Qualifications: Associates degree or two years experience in a related field or an equivalent combination. Experience working with students, young adults, families, or community members in a mentoring, advising, education, social-service, or related capacity. Strong interpersonal and communication skills. Ability to establish and maintain trusting relationships with students and community partners. Demonstrated ability to work independently while also collaborating effectively with a team. Ability to travel regularly to outreach locations, including Montezuma Creek and Monument Valley. Valid driver's license and reliable transportation, as required for community outreach responsibilities. Ability to maintain confidentiality and exercise sound judgment when working with students and sensitive information. Preferred Qualifications: Bachelors degree and two years of experience working in a college-access, student-success, mentoring, advising, recruitment, or retention program. Experience working with students from rural, first-generation, low-income, or historically underserved communities. Familiarity with the educational landscape and communities of southeastern Utah. Experience working with Native American/Indigenous students and communities and demonstrated cultural awareness and respect. Experience building partnerships with schools, tribal organizations, community organizations, or other local agencies. Strong knowledge of college admissions, financial aid, scholarships, and student-support resources. Ability to communicate effectively with students and families from diverse backgrounds. Experience facilitating workshops, presentations, or community events. Required Documents Along with the online application, please attach: Resume to be uploaded at the beginning of your application in the Candidate Profile under “Resume/CV” Cover letter to be typed/pasted at the end of your application **Document size may not exceed 10 MB.** Advertised Salary $18.00 to $20.00 an hour. Commensurate on experience and education. ADA Employees work indoors and are protected from weather and/or contaminants, but not, necessarily, occasional temperature changes. The employee is regularly required to sit and often uses repetitive hand motions. Some travel will be required. University Highlights As Utah’s student-centered, land-grant research university, Utah State University expands opportunity and strengthens communities across the state through learning, discovery, extension, and engagement. Founded in 1888, USU now holds two Carnegie distinctions: R1 status for very high research activity and the Carnegie Community Engagement Classification. Its innovative research and scholarship is grounded in reciprocal partnerships with local, regional, and global communities, advancing knowledge and serving the public good across Utah, the nation, and the world. With over 50 world-class research facilities and the nation’s second-oldest undergraduate research program, USU prepares students to become active, civically engaged leaders who can address critical societal challenges. USU enrolls over 29,800 students, both online and in person at locations throughout the state . Utah State’s 30 locations include a main campus in Logan, Utah, residential campuses in Price and Blanding, and six additional statewide campuses, along with education centers serving every county. USU educates students from all 50 states and more than 80 countries. For over 100 years, USU Extension has served and engaged Utahns, serving every county in the state. Competing at the NCAA Division I level in 16 sports, USU is a proud member of the Pac-12 Conference, which it joined July 1, 2026. The Aggies capped a historic final season in the Mountain West by capturing nine combined regular-season and tournament titles in 2025-26, with five teams advancing to postseason play. This tradition of excellence extends to the classroom, where 232 student-athletes earned academic honors, and to the community, where USU ranked second nationally in Division I service hours, with nine teams placing in the top 10 for community service. Utah State is dedicated to fostering a community where all individuals feel respected, valued, and supported and where diversity of thought and culture are cultivated. We seek to recruit, hire, and retain people from all walks of life who will champion excellence in education, research, discovery, outreach, and service. We believe that promoting a strong sense of community and belonging empowers and engages all members of USU to thrive and be successful. Forbes recognized our commitment to employees when it named Utah State the best employer in Utah in 2023. Learn more about USU. The university provides a Dual Career Assistance Program to support careers for partners who are also seeking employment. Additionally, USU is committed to providing access and a reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities. To request a reasonable accommodation for a disability, contact the university’s ADA coordinator in the Office of Human Resources at 435-797-0122 or hr@usu.edu . *updated 10/26 Notice of Non-discrimination In its programs and activities, including in admissions and employment, Utah State University does not discriminate or tolerate discrimination, including harassment, based on race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other status protected by University policy, Title IX, or any other federal, state, or local law. Utah State University is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate or tolerate discrimination including harassment in employment including in hiring, promotion, transfer, or termination based on race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other status protected by University policy or any other federal, state, or local law. Utah State University does not discriminate in its housing offerings and will treat all persons fairly and equally without regard to race, color, religion, sex, familial status, disability, national origin, source of income, sexual orientation, or gender identity. Additionally, the University endeavors to provide reasonable accommodations when necessary and to ensure equal access to qualified persons with disabilities. In all circumstances, Utah State University follows state and federal laws related to sex-segregated spaces. The following office has been designated to handle inquiries regarding the application of Title IX and its implementing regulations and/or USU’s non-discrimination policies: Civil Rights & Title IX Office Old Main Room 405 1475 Old Main Hill, Logan UT 84322 titleix@usu.edu (435) 797-1266 For further information regarding non-discrimination, please visit crtix.usu.edu or contact: U.S. Department of Education Denver Regional Office 303-844-5695| OCR.Denver@ed.gov U.S. Department of Education Office of Assistant Secretary for Civil Rights 800-421-3481 | OCR@ed.gov *updated 08/2026 Options Apply for this job online Apply Share Share this job Refer Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Application FAQs Software Powered by iCIMS"
