    "https://careers-usu.icims.com/jobs/10758/assistant-professor---clinical-counseling-psychology/job": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Clinical/Counseling Psychology",
      "description": "Assistant Professor - Clinical/Counseling Psychology City Logan Campus Posted Date 4 weeks ago (9/25/2026 12:41 PM) Requisition ID 2026-10758 # of Openings 1 Job Locations US-UT-Logan Category (Portal Searching) Faculty Position Type Benefited Full-Time Job Classification Faculty Percent of Time or Hours per Week 100% College EEJ Coll of Educ Hlth & Human Sci Department Psychology Advertised Salary Commensurate with education and experience, plus excellent benefits. Telework Eligible No Overview The Department of Psychology at Utah State University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor in Clinical or Counseling Health Psychology with a strong background in Clinical Neuroscience whose research examines the intersection of psychological processes, health, and neuroscience. The successful candidate will join our APA-accredited Combined Clinical/Counseling Psychology Ph.D. specialization and contribute to our new undergraduate Behavioral Neuroscience major. We are particularly interested in scholars investigating the neural mechanisms and correlates of clinical and health conditions, as well as interventions designed to improve health and functioning. Relevant areas may include, but are not limited to, chronic health conditions, developmental neuroscience, aging and neurodegenerative disease, neuropsychiatric conditions, neurorehabilitation, neuropsychology, and related areas. We welcome a broad range of methodological approaches across clinical, health, and affective neuroscience, including work with brain stimulation methods, neuroimaging, neurofeedback, and basic research with animal models. We encourage applications from candidates whose work complements existing strengths across Psychology and the broader college. This is a tenure-track, nine-month academic year position anticipated to begin in August 2027. Review of applications will begin November 1, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. The Department of Psychology will consider visa sponsorship. Candidates must have valid U.S. work authorization (e.g., F-1 OPT/STEM OPT, J-1, current H-1B), be eligible for Change of Status (e.g. F-1, J-1), or be eligible for an H-1B transfer. We do not provide sponsorship for initial overseas H-1B filings/fees. Inquiries may be directed to the search committee chair, Dr. JoAnn Tschanz (435-797-1457; joann.tschanz@usu.edu ). Responsibilities The successful candidate will be expected to establish and maintain a nationally visible, externally funded program of research; publish and disseminate high-quality scholarship; mentor doctoral students in the Combined Clinical/Counseling Psychology specialization; contribute to undergraduate and graduate instruction, including the Behavioral Neuroscience curriculum; and participate in department, program, university, and professional service. Qualifications Minimum Qualifications: Ph.D. in Clinical, Counseling, or Combined Psychology from an APA- or CPA-accredited program or international equivalent by the start date; Strong record of research productivity relevant to clinical/health neuroscience; Evidence of potential for an externally funded program of research; Ability to contribute to undergraduate and graduate teaching and mentorship. Preferred Qualifications: Research expertise that complements or extends existing strengths in Psychology and the broader college and contributes to academic programming in health psychology; Potential for interdisciplinary collaboration in areas such as lifespan neuroscience, health and aging, intervention science, or health technology; Demonstrated potential to engage undergraduate and graduate students in research, professional, clinical, community-engaged, or other experiential opportunities that support their academic and career development; Licensed or license-eligible as a psychologist in Utah. Required Documents Along with the online application, please attach: A curriculum vitae to be uploaded at the beginning of your application in the Candidate Profile under “Resume/CV” Up to three examples of scholarly work to be uploaded at the beginning of your application in the Candidate Profile under “Documents 1-10” Names and contact information for three (3) professional references to be entered in the Candidate Profile A cover letter highlighting fit with minimum and preferred qualifications in research, teaching, and mentorship to be typed/pasted at the end of your application **Document size may not exceed 10 MB.* Advertised Salary Commensurate with education and experience, plus excellent benefits. ADA Employees typically work indoors and are protected from weather and/or contaminants, but not, necessarily, occasional temperature changes. College/Department Highlights The Combined Clinical/Counseling Psychology specialization is an APA-accredited doctoral program with a 50-year history of training health service psychologists. The program follows a scientist-practitioner model and has longstanding strengths in health psychology and neuropsychology. Faculty maintain active programs of research and provide doctoral training across clinical and counseling psychology, with students receiving research, teaching, and applied training experiences designed to prepare them for careers in academic, research, and health-service settings. Clinical training and research are supported by the interdisciplinary facilities of the Sorenson Center for Clinical Excellence. The program welcomes colleagues who value collaborative doctoral training and want to contribute to an active community of scholars and educators. The Department of Psychology at Utah State University is a research-intensive department within the Emma Eccles Jones College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences, with strengths spanning basic, translational, and applied psychological science. The department is entering a period of significant faculty growth, with five tenure-track faculty hires planned across four searches in 2026–27. The department includes more than 30 faculty and offers doctoral training through four Ph.D. specializations: Behavior Analysis, Brain and Cognition, Combined Clinical/Counseling Psychology, and School Psychology. Department faculty also contribute to USU’s interdisciplinary Ph.D. program in Neuroscience. At the undergraduate level, the department offers B.S. degrees in Psychology and Behavioral Neuroscience, as well as an award-winning online Psychology program. The department also offers a distance-learning M.Ed. in Professional School Counseling. Faculty maintain active programs of research supported by federal and other external funding and collaborate extensively within the department, across the college, and throughout the university. Existing collaborations connect Psychology faculty with colleagues in areas including biology, human development and family studies, special education and rehabilitation counseling, nursing, speech and hearing sciences, kinesiology, economics, mathematics and statistics, sociology, and natural resources. The department values research that crosses traditional disciplinary boundaries and creates opportunities for collaboration in areas such as behavioral and mental health, neuroscience, health and aging, intervention science, and emerging health technologies. The department is committed to excellence in undergraduate and graduate education, research and scholarship, and service to communities in Utah and beyond. Faculty joining the department during this period of growth will have opportunities to help shape new research collaborations, academic programs, and training initiatives while contributing to established programs with strong records of student mentorship, research productivity, and professional impact. The Emma Eccles Jones College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences is dedicated to excellence in research, teaching, and service activities that advance education, improve health and well-being, and elevate human potential across the lifespan. The college is home to eight academic departments with approximately 200 faculty who are engaged in innovative research and impactful, student-focused graduate and undergraduate education. The college is consistently ranked by US News and World Report as one of the top Graduate Colleges of Education in the country, ranking among the top 30 public institutions of higher education in 2026 and 1st in the state of Utah since 2000. The college’s programs and outreach missions are strengthened by an on-campus elementary laboratory school and five stand-alone centers, including the Sorenson Legacy Foundation Center for Clinical Excellence , Alzheimer’s Disease and Dementia Research Center , Center for the School of the Future , Institute for Disability Research, Policy, & Practice , and National Center for Hearing Assessment & Management . Faculty, students, and staff within the college collaborate with a broad range of community partners to facilitate community-engaged research and provide transformative experiential learning opportunities. Through dedicated teaching, innovative research, and meaningful engagement the Emma Eccles Jones College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences advances education, health, and well-being, enriching the lives of individuals, families, and communities throughout Utah. University Highlights As Utah’s student-centered, land-grant research university, Utah State University expands opportunity and strengthens communities across the state through learning, discovery, extension, and engagement. Founded in 1888, USU now holds two Carnegie distinctions: R1 status for very high research activity and the Carnegie Community Engagement Classification. Its innovative research and scholarship is grounded in reciprocal partnerships with local, regional, and global communities, advancing knowledge and serving the public good across Utah, the nation, and the world. With over 50 world-class research facilities and the nation’s second-oldest undergraduate research program, USU prepares students to become active, civically engaged leaders who can address critical societal challenges. USU enrolls over 29,800 students, both online and in person at locations throughout the state . Utah State’s 30 locations include a main campus in Logan, Utah, residential campuses in Price and Blanding, and six additional statewide campuses, along with education centers serving every county. USU educates students from all 50 states and more than 80 countries. For over 100 years, USU Extension has served and engaged Utahns, serving every county in the state. Competing at the NCAA Division I level in 16 sports, USU is a proud member of the Pac-12 Conference, which it joined July 1, 2026. The Aggies capped a historic final season in the Mountain West by capturing nine combined regular-season and tournament titles in 2025-26, with five teams advancing to postseason play. This tradition of excellence extends to the classroom, where 232 student-athletes earned academic honors, and to the community, where USU ranked second nationally in Division I service hours, with nine teams placing in the top 10 for community service. Utah State is dedicated to fostering a community where all individuals feel respected, valued, and supported and where diversity of thought and culture are cultivated. We seek to recruit, hire, and retain people from all walks of life who will champion excellence in education, research, discovery, outreach, and service. We believe that promoting a strong sense of community and belonging empowers and engages all members of USU to thrive and be successful. Forbes recognized our commitment to employees when it named Utah State the best employer in Utah in 2023. Learn more about USU. The university provides a Dual Career Assistance Program to support careers for partners who are also seeking employment. Additionally, USU is committed to providing access and a reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities. To request a reasonable accommodation for a disability, contact the university’s ADA coordinator in the Office of Human Resources at 435-797-0122 or hr@usu.edu . *updated 10/26 Notice of Non-discrimination In its programs and activities, including in admissions and employment, Utah State University does not discriminate or tolerate discrimination, including harassment, based on race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other status protected by University policy, Title IX, or any other federal, state, or local law. Utah State University is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate or tolerate discrimination including harassment in employment including in hiring, promotion, transfer, or termination based on race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other status protected by University policy or any other federal, state, or local law. Utah State University does not discriminate in its housing offerings and will treat all persons fairly and equally without regard to race, color, religion, sex, familial status, disability, national origin, source of income, sexual orientation, or gender identity. Additionally, the University endeavors to provide reasonable accommodations when necessary and to ensure equal access to qualified persons with disabilities. In all circumstances, Utah State University follows state and federal laws related to sex-segregated spaces. The following office has been designated to handle inquiries regarding the application of Title IX and its implementing regulations and/or USU’s non-discrimination policies: Civil Rights & Title IX Office Old Main Room 405 1475 Old Main Hill, Logan UT 84322 titleix@usu.edu (435) 797-1266 For further information regarding non-discrimination, please visit crtix.usu.edu or contact: U.S. Department of Education Denver Regional Office 303-844-5695| OCR.Denver@ed.gov U.S. Department of Education Office of Assistant Secretary for Civil Rights 800-421-3481 | OCR@ed.gov *updated 08/2026 Options Apply for this job online Apply Share Share this job Refer Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Application FAQs Software Powered by iCIMS"
    },
    "https://careers-usu.icims.com/jobs/10750/assistant-professor---behavior-analysis/job": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Behavior Analysis",
      "description": "Assistant Professor - Behavior Analysis City Logan Campus Posted Date 4 weeks ago (9/25/2026 12:29 PM) Requisition ID 2026-10750 # of Openings 1 Job Locations US-UT-Logan Category (Portal Searching) Faculty Position Type Benefited Full-Time Job Classification Faculty Percent of Time or Hours per Week 100% College EEJ Coll of Educ Hlth & Human Sci Department Psychology Advertised Salary Commensurate with education and experience, plus excellent benefits. Telework Eligible No Overview The Department of Psychology at Utah State University seeks candidates for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Behavior Analysis. We seek to recruit a researcher with a proven track record of conducting contingency management interventions targeting health-related behavior broadly conceived, including addictive behaviors (e.g., substance use disorders, alcohol misuse, smoking, problematic gambling, problematic internet use) and other health domains (e.g., adherence to diabetes monitoring and treatment, exercise, etc.). The position is located on USU’s main campus in Logan, Utah. This position is not eligible for remote work. This is a tenure-track nine-month, academic year appointment anticipated to begin in August 2027. Review of applications will begin October 26, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. The Department of Psychology will consider visa sponsorship. Candidates must have valid U.S. work authorization (e.g., F-1 OPT/STEM OPT, J-1, current H-1B), be eligible for Change of Status (e.g. F-1, J-1), or be eligible for an H-1B transfer. We do not provide sponsorship for initial overseas H-1B filings/fees. Inquiries may be directed to the search committee chair, Dr. Timothy Shahan ( tim.shahan@usu.edu ). Responsibilities Conduct and disseminate research (e.g., establish a research program, publish in peer-reviewed journals). Generate external funding to support your research program (e.g., via government institutions, private foundations, or regional/local agencies). Teach graduate and/or undergraduate classes within areas of expertise and specialization or department needs (including didactic/classroom instruction and applied supervision). Mentor doctoral students within the behavior analysis specialization (e.g., chair dissertation committees, direct a research lab that involves graduate students, support students in progressing toward graduation). Participate in specialization and department governance (e.g., serve on the behavior analysis specialization steering committee, contribute to departmental committees). Contribute service to the department, college, university, and profession. Qualifications Minimum Qualifications: A Ph.D. in Psychology or related field with relevant training in behavior analysis Evidence of excellence in the ability to conduct and disseminate impactful research on health related behavior Evidence of the ability to effectively teach and mentor students. Preferred Qualifications: A research program focused on contingency management for health-related behavior broadly conceived that complements existing NIH-funded research programs within the behavior analysis program and the department while fostering collaborations across behavioral health domains throughout the college A translational research program strongly bridging basic and applied areas of behavior analysis Have interest in and ability to teach courses at the undergraduate and graduate level in basic and advanced behavior analysis, addictions, contingency management, and behavior modification Demonstrated potential to engage undergraduate and graduate students in research, professional, clinical, community-engaged, or other experiential opportunities that support their academic and career development. Required Documents Along with the online application, please attach: A curriculum vitae to be uploaded at the beginning of your application in the Candidate Profile under “Resume/CV” Up to three examples of scholarly work to be uploaded at the beginning of your application in the Candidate Profile under “Documents 1-10” Names and contact information for three (3) professional references to be entered in the Candidate Profile A cover letter describing your qualifications for this position to be typed/pasted at the end of your application **Document size may not exceed 10 MB.* Advertised Salary Commensurate with education and experience, plus excellent benefits. ADA Employees typically work indoors and are protected from weather and/or contaminants, but not, necessarily, occasional temperature changes. College/Department Highlights The Department of Psychology at Utah State University is a research-intensive department within the Emma Eccles Jones College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences, with strengths spanning basic, translational, and applied psychological science. The department is entering a period of significant faculty growth, with five tenure-track faculty hires planned across four searches in 2026–27. The department includes more than 30 faculty and offers doctoral training through four Ph.D. specializations: Behavior Analysis, Brain and Cognition, Combined Clinical/Counseling Psychology, and School Psychology. Department faculty also contribute to USU’s interdisciplinary Ph.D. program in Neuroscience. At the undergraduate level, the department offers B.S. degrees in Psychology and Behavioral Neuroscience, as well as an award-winning online Psychology program. The department also offers a distance-learning M.Ed. in Professional School Counseling. Faculty maintain active programs of research supported by federal and other external funding and collaborate extensively within the department, across the college, and throughout the university. Existing collaborations connect Psychology faculty with colleagues in areas including biology, human development and family studies, special education and rehabilitation counseling, nursing, speech and hearing sciences, kinesiology, economics, mathematics and statistics, sociology, and natural resources. The department values research that crosses traditional disciplinary boundaries and creates opportunities for collaboration in areas such as behavioral and mental health, neuroscience, health and aging, intervention science, and emerging health technologies. The department is committed to excellence in undergraduate and graduate education, research and scholarship, and service to communities in Utah and beyond. Faculty joining the department during this period of growth will have opportunities to help shape new research collaborations, academic programs, and training initiatives while contributing to established programs with strong records of student mentorship, research productivity, and professional impact. The Emma Eccles Jones College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences is dedicated to excellence in research, teaching, and service activities that advance education, improve health and well-being, and elevate human potential across the lifespan. The college is home to eight academic departments with approximately 200 faculty who are engaged in innovative research and impactful, student-focused graduate and undergraduate education. The college is consistently ranked by US News and World Report as one of the top Graduate Colleges of Education in the country, ranking among the top 30 public institutions of higher education in 2026 and 1st in the state of Utah since 2000. The college’s programs and outreach missions are strengthened by an on-campus elementary laboratory school and five stand-alone centers, including the Sorenson Legacy Foundation Center for Clinical Excellence , Alzheimer’s Disease and Dementia Research Center , Center for the School of the Future , Institute for Disability Research, Policy, & Practice , and National Center for Hearing Assessment & Management . Faculty, students, and staff within the college collaborate with a broad range of community partners to facilitate community-engaged research and provide transformative experiential learning opportunities. Through dedicated teaching, innovative research, and meaningful engagement the Emma Eccles Jones College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences advances education, health, and well-being, enriching the lives of individuals, families, and communities throughout Utah. University Highlights As Utah’s student-centered, land-grant research university, Utah State University expands opportunity and strengthens communities across the state through learning, discovery, extension, and engagement. Founded in 1888, USU now holds two Carnegie distinctions: R1 status for very high research activity and the Carnegie Community Engagement Classification. Its innovative research and scholarship is grounded in reciprocal partnerships with local, regional, and global communities, advancing knowledge and serving the public good across Utah, the nation, and the world. With over 50 world-class research facilities and the nation’s second-oldest undergraduate research program, USU prepares students to become active, civically engaged leaders who can address critical societal challenges. USU enrolls over 29,800 students, both online and in person at locations throughout the state . Utah State’s 30 locations include a main campus in Logan, Utah, residential campuses in Price and Blanding, and six additional statewide campuses, along with education centers serving every county. USU educates students from all 50 states and more than 80 countries. For over 100 years, USU Extension has served and engaged Utahns, serving every county in the state. Competing at the NCAA Division I level in 16 sports, USU is a proud member of the Pac-12 Conference, which it joined July 1, 2026. The Aggies capped a historic final season in the Mountain West by capturing nine combined regular-season and tournament titles in 2025-26, with five teams advancing to postseason play. This tradition of excellence extends to the classroom, where 232 student-athletes earned academic honors, and to the community, where USU ranked second nationally in Division I service hours, with nine teams placing in the top 10 for community service. Utah State is dedicated to fostering a community where all individuals feel respected, valued, and supported and where diversity of thought and culture are cultivated. We seek to recruit, hire, and retain people from all walks of life who will champion excellence in education, research, discovery, outreach, and service. We believe that promoting a strong sense of community and belonging empowers and engages all members of USU to thrive and be successful. Forbes recognized our commitment to employees when it named Utah State the best employer in Utah in 2023. Learn more about USU. The university provides a Dual Career Assistance Program to support careers for partners who are also seeking employment. Additionally, USU is committed to providing access and a reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities. To request a reasonable accommodation for a disability, contact the university’s ADA coordinator in the Office of Human Resources at 435-797-0122 or hr@usu.edu . *updated 10/26 Notice of Non-discrimination In its programs and activities, including in admissions and employment, Utah State University does not discriminate or tolerate discrimination, including harassment, based on race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other status protected by University policy, Title IX, or any other federal, state, or local law. Utah State University is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate or tolerate discrimination including harassment in employment including in hiring, promotion, transfer, or termination based on race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other status protected by University policy or any other federal, state, or local law. Utah State University does not discriminate in its housing offerings and will treat all persons fairly and equally without regard to race, color, religion, sex, familial status, disability, national origin, source of income, sexual orientation, or gender identity. Additionally, the University endeavors to provide reasonable accommodations when necessary and to ensure equal access to qualified persons with disabilities. In all circumstances, Utah State University follows state and federal laws related to sex-segregated spaces. The following office has been designated to handle inquiries regarding the application of Title IX and its implementing regulations and/or USU’s non-discrimination policies: Civil Rights & Title IX Office Old Main Room 405 1475 Old Main Hill, Logan UT 84322 titleix@usu.edu (435) 797-1266 For further information regarding non-discrimination, please visit crtix.usu.edu or contact: U.S. Department of Education Denver Regional Office 303-844-5695| OCR.Denver@ed.gov U.S. Department of Education Office of Assistant Secretary for Civil Rights 800-421-3481 | OCR@ed.gov *updated 08/2026 Options Apply for this job online Apply Share Share this job Refer Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Application FAQs Software Powered by iCIMS"
    },
    "https://careers-usu.icims.com/jobs/10739/academic-advisor---concurrent-enrollment/job": {
      "title": "Academic Advisor - Concurrent Enrollment",
      "description": "Academic Advisor - Concurrent Enrollment City Blanding Campus Posted Date 4 weeks ago (9/24/2026 1:50 PM) Requisition ID 2026-10739 # of Openings 1 Job Locations US-UT-Blanding Category (Portal Searching) Student Programs & Services Position Type Benefited Full-Time Job Classification Exempt Percent of Time or Hours per Week 100% of time College Statewide & Online Education Department SW Campuses RES-Blanding Advertised Salary $45,000 - $50,000 Commensurate on experience and education. Plus an excellent benefit package. Telework Eligible No Overview USU Blanding is seeking applicants for an Academic Advisor to advise prospective, current, and former students as part of the NASNTI (Native Americans Servicing Non-Tribal Institutions) II Grant. Incumbents in the Academic Advisor position advise and assist students in the development of academic plans and class schedules as well as assess academic level, attend to student academic inquiries, assist in the resolution of individual academic problems, and advise on academic program changes. Incumbents also prepare students’ graduation applications for Dean approval in DegreeWorks or assist students in semester-by-semester planning in preparation for declaring a major. This is a soft funded position based on funding oppurtunities. Responsibilities Advise students individually and/or with groups regarding academic core and/or departmental requirements, appropriate class selection, academic policies and procedures, and campus resources. Assist students with the identification of long-term goals and career plans. Work to position students to meet requirements for graduation within appropriate periods of time while preparing them to meet future goals. Advise special populations of students within the scope of responsibility, such as new or transfer students, undeclared majors, minority groups, etc., assisting in the exploration of academic programs and focusing on academic direction, procedures, policies, and available resources. Maintain advising records and confidential student evaluation records. Utilize USU information systems to support the delivery of advising services. Serve on appropriate department, college, and/or campus advisory committees. Perform related duties as required or deemed necessary to meet the Academic Advising goals within the Advisor's scope of responsibility. Concurrent Enrollment Advising Conduct regular grade checks and manage academic progress interventions. Assist with planning and attending university or school district events. Collect, track, and report student data Qualifications Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree is required Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Excellent interpersonal and people skills Strong communication skills, being able to communicate effectively with students, staff, and the public from diverse cultural, social, economic, and educational backgrounds. Proficiency with Microsoft Office products, especially Excel and Word. Must be a hands-on, self-directed, organized, and conscientious individual. Strong time management and organizational skills. Effective analytical problem-solving and decision-making skills. Ability to establish and build constructive relationships, especially with students. Attention to detail and quality-focused. Required Documents Along with the online application, please attach: Resume/CV to be uploaded at the beginning of your application in the Candidate Profile under “Resume/CV” Cover Letter to be typed/pasted at the end of your application Contact information for 3 Professional References to be typed at the beginning of the application **Document size may not exceed 10 MB.** Advertised Salary $45,000 - $50,000 Commensurate on experience and education. Plus an excellent benefit package. ADA Employees work indoors and are protected from weather and/or contaminants, but not, necessarily, occasional temperature changes. T he employee is regularly required to sit and often uses repetitive hand motions. Some travel will be required. University Highlights As Utah’s student-centered, land-grant research university, Utah State University expands opportunity and strengthens communities across the state through learning, discovery, extension, and engagement. Founded in 1888, USU now holds two Carnegie distinctions: R1 status for very high research activity and the Carnegie Community Engagement Classification. Its innovative research and scholarship is grounded in reciprocal partnerships with local, regional, and global communities, advancing knowledge and serving the public good across Utah, the nation, and the world. With over 50 world-class research facilities and the nation’s second-oldest undergraduate research program, USU prepares students to become active, civically engaged leaders who can address critical societal challenges. USU enrolls over 29,800 students, both online and in person at locations throughout the state . Utah State’s 30 locations include a main campus in Logan, Utah, residential campuses in Price and Blanding, and six additional statewide campuses, along with education centers serving every county. USU educates students from all 50 states and more than 80 countries. For over 100 years, USU Extension has served and engaged Utahns, serving every county in the state. Competing at the NCAA Division I level in 16 sports, USU is a proud member of the Pac-12 Conference, which it joined July 1, 2026. The Aggies capped a historic final season in the Mountain West by capturing nine combined regular-season and tournament titles in 2025-26, with five teams advancing to postseason play. This tradition of excellence extends to the classroom, where 232 student-athletes earned academic honors, and to the community, where USU ranked second nationally in Division I service hours, with nine teams placing in the top 10 for community service. Utah State is dedicated to fostering a community where all individuals feel respected, valued, and supported and where diversity of thought and culture are cultivated. We seek to recruit, hire, and retain people from all walks of life who will champion excellence in education, research, discovery, outreach, and service. We believe that promoting a strong sense of community and belonging empowers and engages all members of USU to thrive and be successful. Forbes recognized our commitment to employees when it named Utah State the best employer in Utah in 2023. Learn more about USU. The university provides a Dual Career Assistance Program to support careers for partners who are also seeking employment. Additionally, USU is committed to providing access and a reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities. To request a reasonable accommodation for a disability, contact the university’s ADA coordinator in the Office of Human Resources at 435-797-0122 or hr@usu.edu . *updated 10/26 Notice of Non-discrimination In its programs and activities, including in admissions and employment, Utah State University does not discriminate or tolerate discrimination, including harassment, based on race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other status protected by University policy, Title IX, or any other federal, state, or local law. Utah State University is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate or tolerate discrimination including harassment in employment including in hiring, promotion, transfer, or termination based on race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other status protected by University policy or any other federal, state, or local law. Utah State University does not discriminate in its housing offerings and will treat all persons fairly and equally without regard to race, color, religion, sex, familial status, disability, national origin, source of income, sexual orientation, or gender identity. Additionally, the University endeavors to provide reasonable accommodations when necessary and to ensure equal access to qualified persons with disabilities. In all circumstances, Utah State University follows state and federal laws related to sex-segregated spaces. The following office has been designated to handle inquiries regarding the application of Title IX and its implementing regulations and/or USU’s non-discrimination policies: Civil Rights & Title IX Office Old Main Room 405 1475 Old Main Hill, Logan UT 84322 titleix@usu.edu (435) 797-1266 For further information regarding non-discrimination, please visit crtix.usu.edu or contact: U.S. Department of Education Denver Regional Office 303-844-5695| OCR.Denver@ed.gov U.S. Department of Education Office of Assistant Secretary for Civil Rights 800-421-3481 | OCR@ed.gov *updated 08/2026 Options Apply for this job online Apply Share Share this job Refer Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Application FAQs Software Powered by iCIMS"
