    "https://careers-usu.icims.com/jobs/10688/part-time-a-v-tech-event-services/job": {
      "title": "Part-Time A/V Tech Event Services",
      "description": "Part-Time A/V Tech Event Services City Price Campus Posted Date 2 months ago (9/2/2026 5:03 PM) Requisition ID 2026-10688 # of Openings 2 Job Locations US-UT-Price Category (Portal Searching) Other Position Type Non-benefited (hourly) Part-Time Job Classification Non-Benefited Percent of Time or Hours per Week 10-20 hrs/week College Statewide & Online Education Department SW Campuses RES-Eastern-Stud Affrs Advertised Salary $13.50/hour Telework Eligible No Overview USU Eastern is seeking a part-time A/V Tech to support audio and lighting needs during events. This is a hands-on role ideal for individuals with basic experience or interest in theater tech, live sound, or lighting systems. Evening and weekend hours may be required and are based on event schedules. Responsibilities Assist with running lights or sound boards during rehearsals, performances, or special events Assist customers with Audio Visual needs Follow directions from the Geary Supervisor and work collaboratively with other event staff Perform basic equipment checks Help maintain and organize equipment Qualifications Some experience with theater tech (e.g., high school productions, community theater, A/V club, etc.) is preferred Able to learn new equipment and follow technical direction Comfortable working independently and as part of a team Reliable, punctual, and detail-oriented Required Documents Along with the online application, please attach: Resume to be uploaded at the beginning of your application in the Candidate Profile under “Resume/CV” **Document size may not exceed 10 MB.** Advertised Salary $13.50/hour ADA This position involves working in a theater and event setting, often in low-light or loud environments. The role requires attention to detail, the ability to follow instructions, and staying focused during live productions. Employees must have sufficient strength to perform the duties and responsibilities of the position, including lifting up to 25 pounds, walking and standing. Employees must adhere to safety precautions and may be required to use protective equipment. University Highlights As Utah’s student-centered, land-grant research university, Utah State University expands opportunity and strengthens communities across the state through learning, discovery, extension, and engagement. Founded in 1888, USU now holds two Carnegie distinctions: R1 status for very high research activity and the Carnegie Community Engagement Classification. Its innovative research and scholarship is grounded in reciprocal partnerships with local, regional, and global communities, advancing knowledge and serving the public good across Utah, the nation, and the world. With over 50 world-class research facilities and the nation’s second-oldest undergraduate research program, USU prepares students to become active, civically engaged leaders who can address critical societal challenges. USU enrolls over 29,800 students, both online and in person at locations throughout the state . Utah State’s 30 locations include a main campus in Logan, Utah, residential campuses in Price and Blanding, and six additional statewide campuses, along with education centers serving every county. USU educates students from all 50 states and more than 80 countries. For over 100 years, USU Extension has served and engaged Utahns, serving every county in the state. Competing at the NCAA Division I level in 16 sports, USU is a proud member of the Pac-12 Conference, which it joined July 1, 2026. The Aggies capped a historic final season in the Mountain West by capturing nine combined regular-season and tournament titles in 2025-26, with five teams advancing to postseason play. This tradition of excellence extends to the classroom, where 232 student-athletes earned academic honors, and to the community, where USU ranked second nationally in Division I service hours, with nine teams placing in the top 10 for community service. Utah State is dedicated to fostering a community where all individuals feel respected, valued, and supported and where diversity of thought and culture are cultivated. We seek to recruit, hire, and retain people from all walks of life who will champion excellence in education, research, discovery, outreach, and service. We believe that promoting a strong sense of community and belonging empowers and engages all members of USU to thrive and be successful. Forbes recognized our commitment to employees when it named Utah State the best employer in Utah in 2023. Learn more about USU. The university provides a Dual Career Assistance Program to support careers for partners who are also seeking employment. Additionally, USU is committed to providing access and a reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities. To request a reasonable accommodation for a disability, contact the university’s ADA coordinator in the Office of Human Resources at 435-797-0122 or hr@usu.edu . *updated 10/26 Notice of Non-discrimination In its programs and activities, including in admissions and employment, Utah State University does not discriminate or tolerate discrimination, including harassment, based on race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other status protected by University policy, Title IX, or any other federal, state, or local law. Utah State University is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate or tolerate discrimination including harassment in employment including in hiring, promotion, transfer, or termination based on race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other status protected by University policy or any other federal, state, or local law. Utah State University does not discriminate in its housing offerings and will treat all persons fairly and equally without regard to race, color, religion, sex, familial status, disability, national origin, source of income, sexual orientation, or gender identity. Additionally, the University endeavors to provide reasonable accommodations when necessary and to ensure equal access to qualified persons with disabilities. In all circumstances, Utah State University follows state and federal laws related to sex-segregated spaces. The following office has been designated to handle inquiries regarding the application of Title IX and its implementing regulations and/or USU’s non-discrimination policies: Civil Rights & Title IX Office Old Main Room 405 1475 Old Main Hill, Logan UT 84322 titleix@usu.edu (435) 797-1266 For further information regarding non-discrimination, please visit crtix.usu.edu or contact: U.S. Department of Education Denver Regional Office 303-844-5695| OCR.Denver@ed.gov U.S. Department of Education Office of Assistant Secretary for Civil Rights 800-421-3481 | OCR@ed.gov *updated 08/2026 Options Apply for this job online Apply Share Share this job Refer Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Application FAQs Software Powered by iCIMS"
    },
    "https://careers-usu.icims.com/jobs/10681/clinical-director%2c-distributed--veterinary-teaching-program-%28dvtp%29/job": {
      "title": "Clinical Director, Distributed Veterinary Teaching Program (DVTP)",
      "description": "Clinical Director, Distributed Veterinary Teaching Program (DVTP) City Logan Campus Posted Date 2 months ago (9/2/2026 4:39 PM) Requisition ID 2026-10681 # of Openings 1 Job Locations US-UT-Logan Category (Portal Searching) Faculty Position Type Benefited Full-Time Job Classification Faculty Percent of Time or Hours per Week 100% College College of Veterinary Medicine Department Veterinary Clinical & Life Sciences Advertised Salary Commensurate with qualifications and experience Telework Eligible No Overview The Department of Veterinary Clinical and Life Sciences in the College of Veterinary Medicine at Utah State University seeks applicants for the position of Clinical Director of the Distributed Veterinary Teaching Program (DVTP). The Clinical Director is the senior veterinary faculty leader and administrator responsible for the clinical, operational, and personnel functions of the DVTP. Reporting to the Associate Dean for Clinical Programs, the Director directly supervises the DVTP Program Coordinator and Clinical Relations Veterinarians and provides overall leadership for the personnel, processes, partnerships, and quality-assurance activities required to deliver a high-quality distributed clinical year. The Director integrates the student-facing and partner-facing functions of the program. The DVTP Program Coordinator leads student placement coordination, scheduling and logistical support, communications, documentation, records, database maintenance, and general program administration. Clinical Relations Veterinarians support preceptor readiness, preceptor training and development, clinical-site monitoring and troubleshooting, and alignment of clinical learning environments with contemporary standards of veterinary practice. The Clinical Director ensures that these functions operate as a coordinated, accountable, and responsive system. This is a Logan-based position. The Clinical Director's primary office and duty station will be in the Veterinary Medical Education (VME) Building on the Utah State University Logan campus. The position is not designed as a remote or regionally based appointment. The successful candidate will join the College at a pivotal stage in the development of Utah's first four-year Doctor of Veterinary Medicine program. USU's distributed clinical education model places veterinary students in carefully selected veterinary practices, hospitals, diagnostic facilities, shelters, ambulatory services, and other clinical environments where they learn under the supervision of practicing veterinarians and USU faculty. The Clinical Director works closely with the Associate Dean for Clinical Programs, faculty instructors of record, Practice-Ready Advisors, curriculum leaders, and other College personnel. Responsibilities Travel Expectations Travel is an essential function of this position. The Clinical Director is expected to travel to affiliated clinical training sites throughout Utah and the Intermountain Region, with travel anticipated to average approximately 20-30% of work time. Evaluation and monitoring of new and existing clinical training sites; Preceptor orientation, training, development, and support; Relationship development and maintenance with affiliated veterinary instructional partners; Follow-up on clinical-site, preceptor, or student-learning concerns; Quality-assurance and continuous-improvement activities; and Representation of the College at partner and professional meetings. Travel frequency will vary according to program needs and may be greater during periods of clinical-site development, onboarding, or program implementation. Some overnight travel is required. Teaching - 70% Provide leadership for the quality, consistency, and continued development of veterinary clinical education delivered through the Distributed Veterinary Teaching Program. Develop, implement, evaluate, and continuously improve clinical educational experiences in collaboration with College faculty, Clinical Relations Veterinarians, preceptors, and affiliated veterinary instructional partners. Lead the development and implementation of expectations for workplace-based veterinary instruction and clinical supervision. Provide, coordinate, and evaluate initial and ongoing training for veterinary preceptors, including clinical teaching, supervision, feedback, assessment, student safety, and preceptor responsibilities. Mentor veterinarians serving as clinical preceptors and support their development as effective clinical educators. Work with Clinical Relations Veterinarians to support preceptor readiness, clinical-site readiness, clinical teaching quality, contemporary standards of practice, and resolution of site or preceptor concerns. Foster productive relationships with affiliated veterinary instructional partners and other external stakeholders to provide high-quality and appropriately varied clinical learning opportunities. Participate in the evaluation and monitoring of potential and existing clinical training sites and recommend improvements when needed. Collaborate with faculty instructors of record and Practice-Ready Advisors to support consistent assessment of student clinical performance and progressive development toward practice readiness. Help establish and maintain consistent expectations for student supervision, feedback, entrustment, clinical responsibility, and professional behavior across distributed sites. Participate in the development and refinement of clinical-year curriculum, orientation, policies, procedures, and instructional materials. Use student outcomes, preceptor feedback, site evaluations, program data, and other evidence to support continuous quality improvement. Participate in structured didactic, experiential, and clinical instruction as appropriate to the needs of the curriculum. Maintain currency in veterinary clinical practice, veterinary medical education, workplace-based assessment, and emerging approaches to clinical teaching. Contribute to professional productivity appropriate for a clinical faculty appointment, which may include educational scholarship, presentations, continuing education, publications, grants, development of educational resources, or other contributions to veterinary education. Administration and Program Leadership - 20% Directly supervise the DVTP Program Coordinator and Clinical Relations Veterinarians and provide administrative oversight for assigned DVTP support personnel. Establish work priorities, assign responsibilities, manage performance, provide coaching and professional development, and conduct or participate in formal performance evaluations. Provide oversight of the Program Coordinator's supervision of assigned support staff. Lead regular DVTP team meetings and establish clear workflows, accountability, and communication expectations. Ensure effective coordination between student placement, scheduling, documentation, and program-administration functions and clinical partner, preceptor, site-readiness, and quality-assurance functions. Establish priorities for clinical-site development, monitoring, and partner support in alignment with program needs. Manage and assess staffing, workflow, operational, and resource needs and recommend improvements to the Associate Dean for Clinical Programs. Address personnel, programmatic, clinical-site, and preceptor-related issues within the Director's area of responsibility. Ensure timely escalation of significant academic, personnel, safety, accreditation, or partner concerns through appropriate College channels. Build and sustain a team culture characterized by professionalism, responsiveness, accountability, collaboration, and continuous improvement. Ensure that responsibilities delegated across the DVTP team are clearly defined and effectively integrated. Service - 10% Provide leadership and oversight for the operational and clinical functions of the Distributed Veterinary Teaching Program. Work collaboratively with the Associate Dean for Clinical Programs to implement the strategic direction of the clinical program. Support compliance with applicable AVMA Council on Education accreditation standards, policies, and procedures related to distributed clinical education. Participate in the development, review, and implementation of standards for approval and continued monitoring of affiliated veterinary instructional partners. Support College committees responsible for oversight and approval of clinical training sites and experiences. Develop and maintain relationships with veterinarians, practice leaders, professional organizations, veterinary employers, and other stakeholders throughout Utah and the Intermountain Region. Represent the College of Veterinary Medicine in interactions with clinical partners and the broader veterinary profession. Provide veterinary leadership in addressing concerns involving clinical sites, preceptors, clinical standards, or the quality of the student learning environment. Ensure that clinical-site requirements, agreements, training, documentation, data, and operational processes remain aligned across the DVTP. Collaborate with appropriate College and University units on clinical-program development, curriculum, policy, and implementation. Participate in departmental, college, and university governance and other service activities appropriate to faculty rank and appointment. Contribute to efforts that strengthen the visibility, reputation, and professional network of the USU College of Veterinary Medicine. Qualifications Minimum Qualifications Doctor of Veterinary Medicine (DVM), Veterinary Medical Doctor (VMD), or equivalent veterinary degree. Eligibility for appointment to the faculty of Utah State University at the Clinical Assistant Professor level or higher. At least seven years of professional veterinary experience following receipt of the veterinary degree. Current licensure to practice veterinary medicine in a U.S. state or eligibility to obtain appropriate veterinary licensure as required for assigned duties. Demonstrated veterinary professional leadership. Demonstrated supervisory, management, or team-leadership experience. Experience supervising, mentoring, teaching, or supporting the professional development of veterinarians, veterinary students, veterinary technicians, staff, or other members of veterinary teams. Demonstrated ability to organize and coordinate complex programs involving multiple personnel and external partners. Strong interpersonal, organizational, and written and verbal communication skills. Demonstrated ability to build productive professional relationships and address complex or sensitive issues constructively. Ability and willingness to travel approximately 20-30% of work time, including occasional overnight travel. Valid driver’s license to perform essential job responsibilities, such as driving university vehicles and transporting materials Facility with contemporary communication, educational, and information technologies necessary to support a geographically distributed clinical program. Preferred Qualifications Leadership experience in veterinary clinical education, distributed veterinary education, community-based veterinary education, clinical-year administration, or a comparable professional program. Experience directly supervising professional staff or faculty. Significant experience as a veterinary preceptor, clinical educator, medical director, practice owner, practice leader, or clinical program administrator. Experience managing teams with both student-facing and partner-facing responsibilities. Experience developing or managing partnerships between veterinary educational institutions and private, public, nonprofit, or corporate veterinary organizations. Demonstrated expertise in clinical teaching, workplace-based learning, competency-based veterinary education, or assessment of clinical performance. Experience developing or delivering faculty or preceptor-development programming. Practice-management or veterinary organizational leadership experience. Familiarity with AVMA Council on Education accreditation standards and expectations relevant to clinical education. Experience with clinical-program quality assurance or continuous quality improvement. Demonstrated experience managing difficult clinical, personnel, student, or partner-related issues in a professional environment. Experience using educational or clinical-program data to evaluate outcomes and guide improvement. Board certification in an AVMA-recognized veterinary specialty. Evidence of professional productivity in veterinary education or clinical practice. Demonstrated ability to build consensus among individuals and organizations with differing perspectives and priorities. Appointment at Rank Appointment will be made at the rank of Clinical Assistant Professor, Clinical Associate Professor, or Clinical Professor based on the successful candidate's qualifications, professional accomplishments, leadership experience, and record of achievement consistent with Utah State University policy. Candidates considered for appointment at the Clinical Associate Professor or Clinical Professor rank must demonstrate accomplishments and professional stature commensurate with the applicable rank. Required Documents Along with the online application, please attach: Curriculum vitae to be uploaded at the beginning of your application in the Candidate Profile under “Resume/CV” Letter of application describing qualificiations for the position and interest in leading distributed veterinary clinical education at Utah State University to be uploaded at the beginning of your application in the Candidate Profile under “Documents 1-10” Names and contact information for three professional references to be typed at the beginning of the application The letter of application should specifically address the candidate's experience in veterinary leadership, supervision and team management, clinical education or mentorship, development of professional partnerships, and improvement of clinical learning or practice environments. **Document size may not exceed 10 MB.** Advertised Salary Commensurate with qualifications and experience ADA Employees typically work indoors and are protected from weather and/or contaminants, but not, necessarily, occasional temperature changes. College/Department Highlights The successful candidate will join the Utah State University College of Veterinary Medicine during a defining period in its development. Utah State University is establishing the state's first four-year Doctor of Veterinary Medicine program. The College welcomed its inaugural class in 2025 and is developing toward an anticipated class size of approximately 80 veterinary students in subsequent cohorts. A defining feature of the USU veterinary curriculum is its Distributed Veterinary Teaching Program, through which students complete substantial portions of their clinical education in authentic veterinary practice environments. Students learn alongside veterinarians in private practices, specialty and emergency hospitals, ambulatory services, shelters, diagnostic facilities, and other approved clinical settings. The DVTP has progressed from program design to implementation. The College has established processes for approval and monitoring of affiliated veterinary instructional partners, development and training of clinical preceptors, assessment of student clinical performance, documentation of clinical experiences, and continuous evaluation of clinical learning environments. The Clinical Director will have the opportunity not simply to administer an existing program, but to lead and shape the people, systems, partnerships, and educational practices that will define USU's distributed clinical model. The College is seeking a veterinary leader who is energized by the opportunity to connect academic veterinary education with contemporary veterinary practice, lead a multidisciplinary program team, and build strong partnerships with practicing veterinarians who play an essential role in preparing students for successful entry into the profession. University Highlights As Utah’s student-centered, land-grant research university, Utah State University expands opportunity and strengthens communities across the state through learning, discovery, extension, and engagement. Founded in 1888, USU now holds two Carnegie distinctions: R1 status for very high research activity and the Carnegie Community Engagement Classification. Its innovative research and scholarship is grounded in reciprocal partnerships with local, regional, and global communities, advancing knowledge and serving the public good across Utah, the nation, and the world. With over 50 world-class research facilities and the nation’s second-oldest undergraduate research program, USU prepares students to become active, civically engaged leaders who can address critical societal challenges. USU enrolls over 29,800 students, both online and in person at locations throughout the state . Utah State’s 30 locations include a main campus in Logan, Utah, residential campuses in Price and Blanding, and six additional statewide campuses, along with education centers serving every county. USU educates students from all 50 states and more than 80 countries. For over 100 years, USU Extension has served and engaged Utahns, serving every county in the state. Competing at the NCAA Division I level in 16 sports, USU is a proud member of the Pac-12 Conference, which it joined July 1, 2026. The Aggies capped a historic final season in the Mountain West by capturing nine combined regular-season and tournament titles in 2025-26, with five teams advancing to postseason play. This tradition of excellence extends to the classroom, where 232 student-athletes earned academic honors, and to the community, where USU ranked second nationally in Division I service hours, with nine teams placing in the top 10 for community service. Utah State is dedicated to fostering a community where all individuals feel respected, valued, and supported and where diversity of thought and culture are cultivated. We seek to recruit, hire, and retain people from all walks of life who will champion excellence in education, research, discovery, outreach, and service. We believe that promoting a strong sense of community and belonging empowers and engages all members of USU to thrive and be successful. Forbes recognized our commitment to employees when it named Utah State the best employer in Utah in 2023. Learn more about USU. The university provides a Dual Career Assistance Program to support careers for partners who are also seeking employment. Additionally, USU is committed to providing access and a reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities. To request a reasonable accommodation for a disability, contact the university’s ADA coordinator in the Office of Human Resources at 435-797-0122 or hr@usu.edu . *updated 10/26 Notice of Non-discrimination In its programs and activities, including in admissions and employment, Utah State University does not discriminate or tolerate discrimination, including harassment, based on race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other status protected by University policy, Title IX, or any other federal, state, or local law. Utah State University is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate or tolerate discrimination including harassment in employment including in hiring, promotion, transfer, or termination based on race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other status protected by University policy or any other federal, state, or local law. Utah State University does not discriminate in its housing offerings and will "
    },
    "https://careers-usu.icims.com/jobs/10670/outdoor-recreation-research-technician/job": {
      "title": "Outdoor Recreation Research Technician",
      "description": "Outdoor Recreation Research Technician City Logan Campus Posted Date 2 months ago (8/31/2026 12:38 PM) Requisition ID 2026-10670 # of Openings 8 Job Locations US-UT-Logan Category (Portal Searching) Research (non-Faculty) Position Type Non-benefited (hourly) Part-Time Job Classification Non-Benefited Percent of Time or Hours per Week Varied, averaging up to 29 hrs/week College Quinney College of Agric & Nat Res Department Environment & Society Advertised Salary $20/hour Telework Eligible Yes Overview This position will assist with data collection for the USDA Forest Service’s National Visitor Use Monitoring (NVUM) Program. Field technicians will conduct on-site surveys with national forest visitors at recreation access points such as trailheads, forest roads, lakes and reservoirs, resorts, and other recreation sites across the Uinta-Wasatch-Cache and Ashely National Forest. The data collected through these surveys are used by the USDA Forest Service to estimate annual visitation, track recreation patterns and trends, evaluate visitor experiences and recreation infrastructure, and estimate the economic contributions associated with national forest recreation. This position provides hands-on experience in outdoor recreation research, visitor-use monitoring, field data collection, and national forest management. Work schedules are dictated by a randomly generated survey schedule; however, efforts are made to create consistency. Shifts are 6 hours long and fluctuate between 8am-2pm and 2pm-8pm. Drive time to and from survey locations is paid in addition to the 6-hour survey period. Shifts are expected to be elevated in October and decrease during the winter months, then increase again during the spring and summer. Details regarding work schedules will be further discussed with applicants during the interview process. Responsibilities Conduct face-to-face surveys with national forest visitors at designated recreation sites. Interact professionally with a wide variety of forest visitors, sometimes conducting up to 40 interviews per day. Travel independently to survey locations throughout the assigned national forest. Navigate to remote and unfamiliar recreation sites. Work independently for extended periods, including in remote locations and varying weather conditions. Occasionally camp in remote areas for multiple days while completing survey assignments. Follow established survey protocols and accurately collect and record visitor-use data. Maintain a high level of organization, safety awareness, and professionalism while working in the field. Qualifications Motivated, dependable, organized, and self-sufficient. Comfortable approaching and interacting with unfamiliar people. Possess strong communication and interpersonal skills. Ablility to work independently and solve problems in the field. Safety-conscious and capable of exercising good judgment in remote settings. Comfortable spending extended periods alone in remote outdoor environments. Comfortable camping alone when required. Ablility to navigate remote and unfamiliar areas. Required Documents Along with the online application, please attach: Resume to be uploaded at the beginning of your application in the Candidate Profile under “Resume/CV” Cover letter to be typed/pasted at the end of your application **Document size may not exceed 10 MB.** Advertised Salary $20/hour ADA This position involves extensive fieldwork at recreation sites throughout the Uinta-Wasatch-Cache and Ashley National Forests. Employees may work independently for extended periods in remote locations and in a variety of weather and environmental conditions. The position requires regular travel by vehicle, navigating unfamiliar areas, and occasionally camping in remote locations for multiple days. Fieldwork may involve prolonged periods of standing, sitting, walking on uneven terrain, and working outdoors with limited access to facilities. Employees must be able to remain alert and safety-conscious while traveling and working in remote areas. The position also requires sustained interaction with the public, including approaching and conducting interviews with numerous unfamiliar individuals throughout the workday. Employees must be comfortable working independently, following established protocols, managing changing field conditions, solving problems, and exercising sound judgment with limited direct supervision. University Highlights As Utah’s student-centered, land-grant research university, Utah State University expands opportunity and strengthens communities across the state through learning, discovery, extension, and engagement. Founded in 1888, USU now holds two Carnegie distinctions: R1 status for very high research activity and the Carnegie Community Engagement Classification. Its innovative research and scholarship is grounded in reciprocal partnerships with local, regional, and global communities, advancing knowledge and serving the public good across Utah, the nation, and the world. With over 50 world-class research facilities and the nation’s second-oldest undergraduate research program, USU prepares students to become active, civically engaged leaders who can address critical societal challenges. USU enrolls over 29,800 students, both online and in person at locations throughout the state . Utah State’s 30 locations include a main campus in Logan, Utah, residential campuses in Price and Blanding, and six additional statewide campuses, along with education centers serving every county. USU educates students from all 50 states and more than 80 countries. For over 100 years, USU Extension has served and engaged Utahns, serving every county in the state. Competing at the NCAA Division I level in 16 sports, USU is a proud member of the Pac-12 Conference, which it joined July 1, 2026. The Aggies capped a historic final season in the Mountain West by capturing nine combined regular-season and tournament titles in 2025-26, with five teams advancing to postseason play. This tradition of excellence extends to the classroom, where 232 student-athletes earned academic honors, and to the community, where USU ranked second nationally in Division I service hours, with nine teams placing in the top 10 for community service. Utah State is dedicated to fostering a community where all individuals feel respected, valued, and supported and where diversity of thought and culture are cultivated. We seek to recruit, hire, and retain people from all walks of life who will champion excellence in education, research, discovery, outreach, and service. We believe that promoting a strong sense of community and belonging empowers and engages all members of USU to thrive and be successful. Forbes recognized our commitment to employees when it named Utah State the best employer in Utah in 2023. Learn more about USU. The university provides a Dual Career Assistance Program to support careers for partners who are also seeking employment. Additionally, USU is committed to providing access and a reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities. To request a reasonable accommodation for a disability, contact the university’s ADA coordinator in the Office of Human Resources at 435-797-0122 or hr@usu.edu . *updated 10/26 Notice of Non-discrimination In its programs and activities, including in admissions and employment, Utah State University does not discriminate or tolerate discrimination, including harassment, based on race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other status protected by University policy, Title IX, or any other federal, state, or local law. Utah State University is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate or tolerate discrimination including harassment in employment including in hiring, promotion, transfer, or termination based on race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other status protected by University policy or any other federal, state, or local law. Utah State University does not discriminate in its housing offerings and will treat all persons fairly and equally without regard to race, color, religion, sex, familial status, disability, national origin, source of income, sexual orientation, or gender identity. Additionally, the University endeavors to provide reasonable accommodations when necessary and to ensure equal access to qualified persons with disabilities. In all circumstances, Utah State University follows state and federal laws related to sex-segregated spaces. The following office has been designated to handle inquiries regarding the application of Title IX and its implementing regulations and/or USU’s non-discrimination policies: Civil Rights & Title IX Office Old Main Room 405 1475 Old Main Hill, Logan UT 84322 titleix@usu.edu (435) 797-1266 For further information regarding non-discrimination, please visit crtix.usu.edu or contact: U.S. Department of Education Denver Regional Office 303-844-5695| OCR.Denver@ed.gov U.S. Department of Education Office of Assistant Secretary for Civil Rights 800-421-3481 | OCR@ed.gov *updated 08/2026 Options Apply for this job online Apply Share Share this job Refer Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Application FAQs Software Powered by iCIMS"
